256
00:08:10,900 --> 00:08:12,767
- [Tom] No, the door's locked.

257
00:08:12,767 --> 00:08:14,400
(bright orchestra music)

258
00:08:14,400 --> 00:08:15,900
- [Crow] I've seen the
episode where the eggs

259
00:08:15,900 --> 00:08:18,400
come too fast and she starts
putting them in her mouth!

260
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:19,600
(laughing)

261
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:21,833
- [Mike] Some eggs
are sent to solitary.

262
00:08:21,833 --> 00:08:23,867
- [Voiceover] It's a far
cry from the warm breast

263
00:08:23,867 --> 00:08:26,600
of a broody hen to
a modern incubator,

264
00:08:26,600 --> 00:08:30,100
but actually the conditions
are very much the same.

265
00:08:30,100 --> 00:08:33,233
The temperature is held at
99 degrees to approximate

266
00:08:33,233 --> 00:08:34,333
the body heat of the hen.

267
00:08:34,333 --> 00:08:35,567
- [Crow] And this fellow.

268
00:08:35,567 --> 00:08:36,867
- [Voiceover] And a
forced draft gives

269
00:08:36,867 --> 00:08:39,100
plenty of ventilation.

270
00:08:39,100 --> 00:08:42,367
This one unit holds 85,000 eggs.

271
00:08:42,367 --> 00:08:43,900
- [Tom] And one
bathroom for all them.

272
00:08:43,900 --> 00:08:45,233
(laughing)

273
00:08:45,233 --> 00:08:46,800
- [Voiceover] Did you
ever notice the old hen

274
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:49,033
turning the eggs over
occasionally as she sets?

275
00:08:49,033 --> 00:08:50,567
- [Crow] Oh yeah, who hasn't?

276
00:08:50,567 --> 00:08:52,433
- [Voiceover] Here, the same
thing is done automatically.

277
00:08:52,433 --> 00:08:54,767
- [Tom] Ride the wild mouse.

278
00:08:54,767 --> 00:08:56,567
- [Voiceover] And
now we'l wait 21 days

279
00:08:56,567 --> 00:08:58,033
for things to happen.

280
00:08:58,033 --> 00:08:59,500
- [Crow] Oh, you can't
wait for things to happen,

281
00:08:59,500 --> 00:09:02,267
young man, you've got
to make them happen!

282
00:09:02,267 --> 00:09:04,767
- [Voiceover] But thanks to
some very special photography

283
00:09:04,767 --> 00:09:06,400
we can see what's happening.

284
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:09,200
- [Mike] Oh, this is
gonna be hot, hot, hot.

285
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:10,400
- [Voiceover] By the
end of the third day,

286
00:09:10,400 --> 00:09:12,533
the embryo in the egg
has begun to form.

287
00:09:12,533 --> 00:09:13,800
- [Tom] Flock, calling all sons.

288
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:15,867
- [Crow] Hey, I'm
trying to sleep in here.

289
00:09:17,267 --> 00:09:18,767
- [Voiceover] Another
three days, and the heart

290
00:09:18,767 --> 00:09:20,000
has started to beat.

291
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:21,233
- [Mike] Say, can I
have a bigger egg?

292
00:09:21,233 --> 00:09:23,333
- [Tom] That is
one bloodshot eye.

293
00:09:23,333 --> 00:09:25,100
- [Mike] Wow.

294
00:09:25,100 --> 00:09:26,567
- [Voiceover] After
10 days, you can see

295
00:09:26,567 --> 00:09:29,000
the chick itself
beginning to take shape.

296
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,033
- [Crow] Oh, wait,
that's my silly putty.

297
00:09:31,933 --> 00:09:33,767
(mysterious orchestral music)

298
00:09:33,767 --> 00:09:35,500
- [Voiceover] A few
more days and that mass

299
00:09:35,500 --> 00:09:37,733
has some resemblance
to a baby chick.

300
00:09:37,733 --> 00:09:39,000
- [Mike] If you kind of squint.

301
00:09:40,567 --> 00:09:41,800
- [Crow] I'll dry eye.

302
00:09:41,800 --> 00:09:45,000
(hiccuping)
(giggling)

303
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,100
- [Voiceover] And finally,
the fully-developed chick

304
00:09:47,100 --> 00:09:49,633
is ready to start
breaking out of its shell.

305
00:09:49,633 --> 00:09:51,633
- [Tom] Sticks of dynamite
are arranged carefully

306
00:09:51,633 --> 00:09:53,167
around the perimeter.

307
00:09:54,333 --> 00:09:55,633
- [Voiceover] At this
stage, Mother Nature

308
00:09:55,633 --> 00:09:58,400
gives it a sharp spike
on the end of its beak,

309
00:09:58,400 --> 00:10:00,733
sharp enough to poke a
hole through the shell.

310
00:10:02,167 --> 00:10:04,167
Little by little, the
chick works its way

311
00:10:04,167 --> 00:10:05,400
around the shell.

312
00:10:05,400 --> 00:10:06,733
- [Mike] Hold it, I'll
be out in a minute.

313
00:10:06,733 --> 00:10:10,767
It'll just take a little
longer than I thought.

314
00:10:10,767 --> 00:10:13,767
- Come on down and
meet everybody.

315
00:10:13,767 --> 00:10:16,267
(laughing)

316
00:10:16,267 --> 00:10:17,733
- [Voiceover] Finally
the end of the shell

317
00:10:17,733 --> 00:10:19,533
is weakened enough so the
chick can push it out.

318
00:10:19,533 --> 00:10:21,467
- [Tom] Yeah, right,
you push it out.

319
00:10:21,467 --> 00:10:22,833
Geez, this is hard.

320
00:10:22,833 --> 00:10:25,500
(grunting)

321
00:10:25,500 --> 00:10:26,333
- [Mike] I hate it
when people tape

322
00:10:26,333 --> 00:10:27,300
their own deliveries.

323
00:10:28,833 --> 00:10:31,500
- [Tom] Aren't there supposed
to be pantyhose in there?

324
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:33,000
- [Mike] Wait, I'm
coming, I'm not done.

325
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:33,833
Ow.

326
00:10:35,733 --> 00:10:37,933
(grunting)

327
00:10:39,100 --> 00:10:40,333
- [Voiceover] And after
a lot of struggling

328
00:10:40,333 --> 00:10:43,600
it tumbles out,
exhausted, but free.

329
00:10:43,600 --> 00:10:45,867
- Oh, oh, oh, what
did I do last night?

330
00:10:45,867 --> 00:10:47,167
- [Voiceover] It'll rest
now for several hours

331
00:10:47,167 --> 00:10:50,033
and then get up on shaky
legs and begin to live.

332
00:10:50,033 --> 00:10:52,033
- [Mike] For a few
weeks, only to be eaten.

333
00:10:54,567 --> 00:10:55,800
- [Voiceover]
Full-time poultrymen

334
00:10:55,800 --> 00:10:57,267
who have been in the
business for many years

335
00:10:57,267 --> 00:10:59,600
say they still get
a mighty kick out of

336
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:01,567
the miracle of the
birth of a baby chick.

337
00:11:01,567 --> 00:11:02,900
- [Tom] Hey guys, it's God.

338
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:05,733
- [Voiceover] The ratio
of pullets and cockerels

339
00:11:05,733 --> 00:11:07,567
among chicks is about 50/50.

340
00:11:07,567 --> 00:11:09,067
- Great, what are those?

341
00:11:09,067 --> 00:11:11,000
- [Voiceover] Of course, you
may order straight run chicks.

342
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,367
Then again, you may
order all pullets.

343
00:11:13,367 --> 00:11:14,633
- [Mike] Life is
great, it stretches

344
00:11:14,633 --> 00:11:16,600
out in front of me
like an eternity.

345
00:11:16,600 --> 00:11:18,133
- [Voiceover] Sexing the
chicks, or separating

346
00:11:18,133 --> 00:11:21,367
the male from the females is
a highly-specialized trade.

347
00:11:21,367 --> 00:11:22,733
- [Tom] Yeah, for pervs.

348
00:11:22,733 --> 00:11:24,200
Whoa, Milton Merl, there.

349
00:11:24,200 --> 00:11:26,433
(laughs)

350
00:11:26,433 --> 00:11:28,767
- [Voiceover] Called for
long experience and training,

351
00:11:28,767 --> 00:11:30,533
but the experts seldom miss.

352
00:11:31,900 --> 00:11:34,500
They can't afford to miss,
because when you order pullets,

353
00:11:34,500 --> 00:11:37,567
you want all pullets, not
a few cockerels mixed in.

354
00:11:37,567 --> 00:11:39,400
- [Mike] Hey, why
are we screeching?

355
00:11:40,767 --> 00:11:42,267
(chirping)
- Garage sale.

356
00:11:42,267 --> 00:11:43,767
Goodwill.

357
00:11:43,767 --> 00:11:44,700
Save for the kids.

358
00:11:45,833 --> 00:11:48,633
(bright orchestra music)

359
00:11:48,633 --> 00:11:50,333
- [Mike] It's nice,
you know it's small,

360
00:11:50,333 --> 00:11:51,400
the walls are neutral.

361
00:11:53,133 --> 00:11:54,967
- [Tom] Oh, hi, Cindy, I'm
so glad I'm in your group.

362
00:11:54,967 --> 00:11:56,467
This is gonna be the fun group.

363
00:11:57,467 --> 00:11:58,700
(chirping)

364
00:11:58,700 --> 00:12:00,667
- [Crow] 40-piece
chicken nuggets to go.

365
00:12:00,667 --> 00:12:02,067
- [Voiceover] "But wait a
minute.", you may be saying.

366
00:12:02,067 --> 00:12:03,500
- [Crow] Why am I watching this?

367
00:12:03,500 --> 00:12:04,733
- [Voiceover] Can those
chicks, just out of the shell,

368
00:12:04,733 --> 00:12:07,967
be sent, without food,
on trips of a day,

369
00:12:07,967 --> 00:12:09,633
two days, even three?

370
00:12:09,633 --> 00:12:10,933
- [Tom] You bet.

371
00:12:10,933 --> 00:12:12,733
- [Voiceover] Indeed they can.

372
00:12:12,733 --> 00:12:15,033
Nature thought about that, too.

373
00:12:15,033 --> 00:12:17,133
The baby chick has
within its little body

374
00:12:17,133 --> 00:12:22,000
enough unabsorbed yoke, to
nourish it for 72 hours,

375
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,700
three days and nights of travel,

376
00:12:24,700 --> 00:12:26,800
with each chick
carrying its own lunch.

377
00:12:26,800 --> 00:12:28,533
- [Mike] Then they
turn on each other.

378
00:12:30,700 --> 00:12:33,200
- [Voiceover] Nevertheless,
speed is essential

379
00:12:33,200 --> 00:12:34,767
and it's here that
the motortruck

380
00:12:34,767 --> 00:12:37,100
plays such a big part
in poultry raising.

381
00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:39,000
- I said speed is essential!

382
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:40,300
- [Voiceover] In fact, the
industry couldn't exist

383
00:12:40,300 --> 00:12:43,200
as it does today without
motor transportation

384
00:12:43,200 --> 00:12:45,633
and the petroleum
industry that supplies

385
00:12:45,633 --> 00:12:48,333
the quality fuel and
lubricants that make

386
00:12:48,333 --> 00:12:51,067
this high-speed
transportation possible.

387
00:12:51,067 --> 00:12:52,767
- [Tom] The unholy
alliance between big oil

388
00:12:52,767 --> 00:12:54,267
and big chickens.

389
00:12:54,267 --> 00:12:56,300
- [Mike] That guy's escaping
disguised as a chicken.

390
00:12:56,300 --> 00:12:57,600
- [Crow] Chickens!

391
00:12:58,833 --> 00:13:00,733
- [Voiceover] But to get
back to our baby chicks.

392
00:13:00,733 --> 00:13:02,200
- [Mike] I'm full.

393
00:13:02,200 --> 00:13:03,400
- [Voiceover] This made a
cute picture, but it's really

394
00:13:03,400 --> 00:13:06,167
not the scientific
way to handle chicks.

395
00:13:06,167 --> 00:13:08,700
- [Mike] How many
are you sitting on?

396
00:13:08,700 --> 00:13:10,900
- I've been a little
nuts this Easter.

397
00:13:10,900 --> 00:13:12,733
- [Voiceover] Their immediate
destination after leaving

398
00:13:12,733 --> 00:13:15,333
the incubator is
the brooder house.

399
00:13:15,333 --> 00:13:17,100
- [Mike] Designed by
Frank Lloyd Wright.

400
00:13:19,200 --> 00:13:20,467
- [Voiceover] Here
a stove is needed

401
00:13:20,467 --> 00:13:23,167
to keep the temperature
up around 95 degrees

402
00:13:23,167 --> 00:13:24,900
for the first week.

403
00:13:24,900 --> 00:13:26,433
Almost any kind
of stove will do,

404
00:13:26,433 --> 00:13:28,767
one of those most common
being a kerosene stove.

405
00:13:28,767 --> 00:13:30,300
- Here I come!

406
00:13:30,300 --> 00:13:32,400
- [Tom] Oh, they mingle, get
to know each other a little.

407
00:13:32,400 --> 00:13:34,333
- [Voiceover] The circular
guard is to keep the chicks

408
00:13:34,333 --> 00:13:36,100
near the heat and
prevent floor drafts.

409
00:13:36,100 --> 00:13:37,367
- [Tom] There are
concerts at the gazebo.

410
00:13:37,367 --> 00:13:38,933
- [Voiceover] For a
few days, the litter

411
00:13:38,933 --> 00:13:41,033
should be covered with
paper, so the chicks

412
00:13:41,033 --> 00:13:42,500
don't eat it.

413
00:13:42,500 --> 00:13:45,833
They need plenty of food
and lots and lots of water.

414
00:13:45,833 --> 00:13:47,433
- Individual
counseling is provided.

415
00:13:47,433 --> 00:13:49,500
- [Voiceover] From now
on, their main job in life

416
00:13:49,500 --> 00:13:51,300
is to eat and grow.

417
00:13:51,300 --> 00:13:52,300
- [Crow] Eat and grow forever.

418
00:13:54,467 --> 00:13:56,167
- [Voiceover] After the
first few days, the paper

419
00:13:56,167 --> 00:13:57,467
can be removed.

420
00:13:57,467 --> 00:13:58,967
- [Crow] Except for
the sports section.

421
00:13:58,967 --> 00:14:00,500
- [Voiceover] Floor should
be covered with litter

422
00:14:00,500 --> 00:14:02,367
to keep the floor warm
and absorb the droppings.

423
00:14:03,767 --> 00:14:07,467
Peat moss, fine-cut straw,
peanut shells, brown corncobs

424
00:14:07,467 --> 00:14:10,433
or anything that will
pack rather closely

425
00:14:10,433 --> 00:14:11,600
is good for litter.

426
00:14:11,600 --> 00:14:13,400
- [Crow] Cigarette
butts, hair, beer cans.

427
00:14:14,600 --> 00:14:18,000
(upbeat synth pop music)

428
00:14:20,867 --> 00:14:23,367
- [Voiceover] After the chicks
are two or three weeks old,

429
00:14:23,367 --> 00:14:24,833
they can be allowed
out of doors,

430
00:14:24,833 --> 00:14:26,067
if the weather's good.

431
00:14:26,067 --> 00:14:27,667
- [Tom] And if they've
completed their lessons.

432
00:14:29,567 --> 00:14:30,733
- [Voiceover] At from
eight to 10 weeks,

433
00:14:30,733 --> 00:14:32,800
the pullets being reared
for egg production

434
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:35,633
are ready to be transferred
from the brooder house

435
00:14:35,633 --> 00:14:36,933
to the range.

436
00:14:36,933 --> 00:14:38,400
- And you know what that means.

437
00:14:38,400 --> 00:14:40,300
(laughing)

438
00:14:40,300 --> 00:14:42,833
- [Voiceover] Range life
is good for chickens.

439
00:14:42,833 --> 00:14:45,167
- Huh, what, what?
- What's going on?

440
00:14:45,167 --> 00:14:47,833
- [Tom] What, really, no, you?

441
00:14:47,833 --> 00:14:49,900
- [Voiceover] Range shelter
should be fairly small

442
00:14:49,900 --> 00:14:52,100
holding not more than 100 birds.

443
00:14:52,100 --> 00:14:53,833
- [Tom] Or migrant workers.

444
00:14:53,833 --> 00:14:55,800
- [Voiceover] The shelter
protects them from the sun,

445
00:14:55,800 --> 00:14:57,333
and gives them a
safe place to roost

446
00:14:57,333 --> 00:14:58,667
out of the way of rodents.

447
00:14:58,667 --> 00:14:59,500
- Rodents?
- Rodents?

448
00:14:59,500 --> 00:15:00,933
(yelling)

449
00:15:00,933 --> 00:15:03,567
- [Mike] Get out, go, go!

450
00:15:03,567 --> 00:15:05,700
- [Voiceover] Proper
diet and plenty of water

451
00:15:05,700 --> 00:15:06,967
are required.

452
00:15:06,967 --> 00:15:08,200
- [Tom] Heavens, I'm
so fat, I just look

453
00:15:08,200 --> 00:15:09,333
at chicken feed
and I gain weight.

454
00:15:09,333 --> 00:15:10,267
(laughs)

455
00:15:10,267 --> 00:15:11,900
- Does this taste funny to you?

456
00:15:11,900 --> 00:15:13,567
- [Voiceover] A good
grass sod is a must

457
00:15:13,567 --> 00:15:15,400
to give the birds
an abundant supply

458
00:15:15,400 --> 00:15:17,033
of fresh, tender leaves.

459
00:15:17,033 --> 00:15:19,000
- [Mike] A selection of
ciders and spring water

460
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,200
should be kept on hand.

461
00:15:21,200 --> 00:15:22,567
- [Crow] Normandy.

462
00:15:22,567 --> 00:15:23,833
- [Voiceover] Rain
shelters ought to be spaced

463
00:15:23,833 --> 00:15:26,667
about 75 to 100 feet
apart, so then hens

464
00:15:26,667 --> 00:15:30,333
won't go wondering off to
gossip with other flocks.

465
00:15:30,333 --> 00:15:31,600
You know how chickens are.

466
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:34,200
- [Tom] Yeah, they own
everything, bastards.

467
00:15:34,200 --> 00:15:36,133
- [Voiceover] Oh, it's
a fine life, this life

468
00:15:36,133 --> 00:15:38,467
on the range, but
like all good things

469
00:15:38,467 --> 00:15:40,400
it must come to an end.

470
00:15:40,400 --> 00:15:41,867
- [Tom] Time to die.

471
00:15:41,867 --> 00:15:43,133
- [Voiceover] And when they
are about six months old

472
00:15:43,133 --> 00:15:45,033
the pullets are ready to
move to the laying house

473
00:15:45,033 --> 00:15:47,200
to fulfill one of their
main functions in life.

474
00:15:47,200 --> 00:15:49,700
- [Crow] I can't, I've
got a tiny headache.

475
00:15:51,033 --> 00:15:52,267
- [Voiceover] Remember
the old hen house?

476
00:15:52,267 --> 00:15:53,500
- [Tom] The one with the rats.

477
00:15:53,500 --> 00:15:55,533
- [Voiceover] It's now
a hotel, a pullet hotel.

478
00:15:55,533 --> 00:15:57,500
- [Tom] Rooms by the hour.

479
00:15:57,500 --> 00:15:59,833
- [Voiceover] Every room
with an outside exposure.

480
00:15:59,833 --> 00:16:02,200
- [Crow] Joey "The
Cockscomb" Tortelli.

481
00:16:02,200 --> 00:16:04,333
Ricky "The Bathroom"
Shah Vitello.

482
00:16:04,333 --> 00:16:06,300
Jimmy "Crazy Cock" Cuccini.

483
00:16:06,300 --> 00:16:08,800
- [Voiceover] Or if you
prefer the bungalow-type,

484
00:16:08,800 --> 00:16:10,533
we've got that too,

485
00:16:10,533 --> 00:16:12,333
400 feet of it.

486
00:16:12,333 --> 00:16:14,900
All together, room for
thousands of guests.

487
00:16:14,900 --> 00:16:16,133
- [Mike] Or chickens, typically.

488
00:16:16,133 --> 00:16:17,833
- [Voiceover] Seriously,
housing your chickens

489
00:16:17,833 --> 00:16:22,300
is important, whether they're
egg or meat producers,

490
00:16:22,300 --> 00:16:25,300
they aught to have three or
four square feet per bird,

491
00:16:25,300 --> 00:16:26,700
depending on the breed.

492
00:16:26,700 --> 00:16:28,833
- [Crow] Open your
hymnals to number 325.

493
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:31,167
- [Voiceover] They got
to have roosts, too,

494
00:16:31,167 --> 00:16:33,300
but they huddle
pretty close together

495
00:16:33,300 --> 00:16:35,967
and two or three birds can
perch on one foot of space.

496
00:16:35,967 --> 00:16:40,133
- [Tom] Everybody, soylent
green is made from chickens!

497
00:16:41,333 --> 00:16:42,600
- [Voiceover] One of the
most important advances

498
00:16:42,600 --> 00:16:45,933
every made in poultry
raising is the trapnest.

499
00:16:45,933 --> 00:16:48,733
The bird can enter the
next easily to lay her egg,

500
00:16:48,733 --> 00:16:51,833
but she can't get out again,
until you let her out.

501
00:16:51,833 --> 00:16:53,733
- [Tom] There's not
point, it's just funny.

502
00:16:53,733 --> 00:16:55,700
- [Voiceover] This simple
device permits you to know

503
00:16:55,700 --> 00:16:58,000
which birds lay which eggs,

504
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,933
and to keep a record of
each hen's egg production.

505
00:17:00,933 --> 00:17:03,100
- [Crow] Which seems
excessively anal-retentive.

506
00:17:03,100 --> 00:17:05,167
- [Voiceover] The whole secret
of profitable chicken raising

507
00:17:05,167 --> 00:17:07,400
is to make them produce
more than they cost to feed.

508
00:17:07,400 --> 00:17:10,100
- [Tom] You think I can wear
these pants out tonight?

509
00:17:12,533 --> 00:17:14,800
- [Voiceover] A hen that
lays 210 eggs a year

510
00:17:14,800 --> 00:17:17,333
and eats 70 pounds of
feed, is giving you

511
00:17:17,333 --> 00:17:19,433
three eggs for every
pound you feed her.

512
00:17:19,433 --> 00:17:20,567
- [Mike] She will live.

513
00:17:20,567 --> 00:17:21,500
- [Voiceover] Keep that one.

514
00:17:21,500 --> 00:17:22,900
- [Crow] She's worth millions.

515
00:17:24,067 --> 00:17:25,900
- [Voiceover] But if she
eats 70 pounds of feed

516
00:17:25,900 --> 00:17:28,567
and only lays 70 eggs a year,

517
00:17:28,567 --> 00:17:29,933
you better send
her to the market

518
00:17:29,933 --> 00:17:31,333
or to your dinner table.

519
00:17:31,333 --> 00:17:33,267
- [Crow] Or put a
warning slip on her desk.

520
00:17:34,667 --> 00:17:36,100
- [Voiceover] And it's
the trapnest that lets you

521
00:17:36,100 --> 00:17:38,500
keep record of how well
each hen is laying.

522
00:17:38,500 --> 00:17:40,233
- [Mike] I'll have an egg
tomorrow, man, I swear.

523
00:17:40,233 --> 00:17:41,467
- [Voiceover] It's men
like this, too, that help

524
00:17:41,467 --> 00:17:43,167
in developing the
chicken of tomorrow.

525
00:17:43,167 --> 00:17:44,867
- [Tom] There, go
have little fun.

526
00:17:46,300 --> 00:17:47,567
- [Voiceover] You can't
take it for granted

527
00:17:47,567 --> 00:17:49,667
that every hen is
earning her keep,

528
00:17:49,667 --> 00:17:51,900
even though laying an
egg aught to be easy

529
00:17:51,900 --> 00:17:53,633
for any chicken.

530
00:17:53,633 --> 00:17:56,000
- [Voiceover] That's
what you think, big boy.

531
00:17:57,033 --> 00:17:58,900
- That was weird.
- Yeah.

532
00:17:58,900 --> 00:18:02,200
- [Voiceover] Well, anyhow,
the trapnest's the answer

533
00:18:02,200 --> 00:18:04,833
to discovering which
are the lazy hens,

534
00:18:04,833 --> 00:18:06,800
and this is a good
place to point out

535
00:18:06,800 --> 00:18:08,267
a few facts about eggs.

536
00:18:08,267 --> 00:18:10,533
- [Mike] Stop throwing
them at my car.

537
00:18:10,533 --> 00:18:12,433
- [Voiceover] The temperature
of an egg when laid

538
00:18:12,433 --> 00:18:14,933
is over 100 degrees.

539
00:18:14,933 --> 00:18:16,433
- [Tom] Oh it's true.

540
00:18:16,433 --> 00:18:17,933
- [Voiceover] Every minute
it's left in a hot nest

541
00:18:17,933 --> 00:18:20,833
in a hot hen house takes
away some of its moisture

542
00:18:20,833 --> 00:18:22,067
and freshness.

543
00:18:22,067 --> 00:18:23,700
- [Crow] So put your
mouth under a chicken.

544
00:18:24,967 --> 00:18:26,700
- [Voiceover] Gather
your eggs often,

545
00:18:26,700 --> 00:18:28,200
three or four times a day.

546
00:18:28,200 --> 00:18:30,533
- [Crow] Makes sure to put
them all in one basket.

547
00:18:31,533 --> 00:18:33,867
(bright orchestra music)

548
00:18:33,867 --> 00:18:35,433
- [Voiceover] And they'll
cool out twice as fast

549
00:18:35,433 --> 00:18:37,900
in a wire basket, as in a pail.

550
00:18:37,900 --> 00:18:40,700
- [Tom] These are headed
for the driving range.

551
00:18:40,700 --> 00:18:42,200
- [Voiceover] Clean eggs
bring a better price

552
00:18:42,200 --> 00:18:45,100
than dirty ones and when
you gather them often,

553
00:18:45,100 --> 00:18:46,867
they don't get a
chance to get dirty.

554
00:18:46,867 --> 00:18:50,133
- [Tom] Unless filthy
Fred, there, collects them.

555
00:18:50,133 --> 00:18:51,567
- [Voiceover] The secret
of profitable eggs

556
00:18:51,567 --> 00:18:54,500
is to cool them quickly
and keep them cool.

557
00:18:54,500 --> 00:18:55,867
- [Tom] Yeah, I'm cool, baby.

558
00:18:55,867 --> 00:18:57,567
- [Voiceover] And
keep them clean.

559
00:18:57,567 --> 00:18:59,933
- [Crow] Lick your eggs or
have a friend lick them.

560
00:18:59,933 --> 00:19:01,700
(laughing)

561
00:19:01,700 --> 00:19:02,800
- [Voiceover] When
you've got as many birds

562
00:19:02,800 --> 00:19:04,700
to look after as this hatchery

563
00:19:04,700 --> 00:19:07,433
your pretty receptive
to labor-saving devices.

564
00:19:07,433 --> 00:19:08,900
- [Tom] Like wagons.

565
00:19:08,900 --> 00:19:11,900
- [Voiceover] And this carrier
system is one of the best.

566
00:19:11,900 --> 00:19:13,633
It runs the length
of the building

567
00:19:13,633 --> 00:19:16,067
and is used to carry feed
to the different pens.

568
00:19:17,200 --> 00:19:19,433
It can be used also for
gathering up manure.

569
00:19:19,433 --> 00:19:21,600
Saves a lot of
back-breaking work.

570
00:19:21,600 --> 00:19:24,167
- [Tom] Hey, pal, feed me,
then clean up my poops.

571
00:19:25,933 --> 00:19:27,167
- [Crow] There's
your appetizers.

572
00:19:27,167 --> 00:19:30,000
I'll be back to get your
drink orders, Ladies.

573
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:31,100
- [Voiceover] Another
labor saver is

574
00:19:31,100 --> 00:19:33,433
the automatic watering trough.

575
00:19:33,433 --> 00:19:35,500
It makes certain there
is always fresh water

576
00:19:35,500 --> 00:19:36,833
for the birds to drink.

577
00:19:36,833 --> 00:19:39,233
- [Mike] We're all
wearing Rembrandt hats.

578
00:19:39,233 --> 00:19:40,633
- [Voiceover] Still
anther handy gadget

579
00:19:40,633 --> 00:19:41,700
is this grading machine.

580
00:19:41,700 --> 00:19:42,933
- [Crow] Grading!

581
00:19:42,933 --> 00:19:44,300
- [Voiceover] You
feed the eggs in

582
00:19:44,300 --> 00:19:45,533
and the machine
separates them by weight.

583
00:19:45,533 --> 00:19:47,100
- [Tom] Uh, that
one's clean, Roy.

584
00:19:48,333 --> 00:19:49,400
- [Mike] Eggs are complicated.

585
00:19:49,400 --> 00:19:51,167
They should cost like $100 each.

586
00:19:52,267 --> 00:19:53,767
- [Voiceover] The first
group will average

587
00:19:53,767 --> 00:19:56,400
24 ounces to the dozen, which
is the most profitable size.

588
00:19:56,400 --> 00:19:59,867
The next, 23 and a half ounces.

589
00:19:59,867 --> 00:20:02,133
The next, 23 and so on.

590
00:20:02,133 --> 00:20:04,100
- [Tom] Some eggs
don't even exist.

591
00:20:05,667 --> 00:20:08,600
- [Mike] So you can see why
egg farming is so fascinating.

592
00:20:09,767 --> 00:20:11,400
- [Voiceover] Besides
being a good egg-layer,

593
00:20:11,400 --> 00:20:12,633
the chicken of tomorrow will be

594
00:20:12,633 --> 00:20:14,000
an improved meat producer.

595
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:15,267
- [Mike] It's
Goofus and Gallant.

596
00:20:15,267 --> 00:20:16,633
- [Voiceover] Here's an
example of the progress

597
00:20:16,633 --> 00:20:17,967
that's been made already.

598
00:20:17,967 --> 00:20:19,200
- [Crow] This could
be a drumstick.

599
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:20,533
This is the number to call.

600
00:20:20,533 --> 00:20:22,233
- [Voiceover] Notice how
breeding has increased

601
00:20:22,233 --> 00:20:23,900
the amount of meat
on the breast.

602
00:20:23,900 --> 00:20:26,167
- [Tom] Hey, hey,
hey, Fat Albert.

603
00:20:26,167 --> 00:20:27,267
- [Voiceover] Look
at that drumstick.

604
00:20:27,267 --> 00:20:29,500
- [Crow] Full of salmonella.

605
00:20:29,500 --> 00:20:30,567
- [Voiceover] This
bird was fattened

606
00:20:30,567 --> 00:20:32,567
in the same length of time

607
00:20:32,567 --> 00:20:35,767
and on the same amount
of feed as the other one.

608
00:20:35,767 --> 00:20:36,933
Make your own guess as to which

609
00:20:36,933 --> 00:20:38,400
is the more profitable to raise.

610
00:20:38,400 --> 00:20:40,233
- [Tom] Aren't chickens
with good self-esteem

611
00:20:40,233 --> 00:20:41,733
equally important?

612
00:20:41,733 --> 00:20:42,967
- [Voiceover] And when
the chicken of tomorrow

613
00:20:42,967 --> 00:20:45,700
gets to the dinner
table, its advantages

614
00:20:45,700 --> 00:20:47,233
are still more apparent.

615
00:20:47,233 --> 00:20:49,433
- [Mike] These chickens
were smoking in bed.

616
00:20:51,167 --> 00:20:54,067
- [Crow] May I have a piece
of my own white meat, please?

617
00:20:56,433 --> 00:20:59,100
- [Tom] Oh, thanks for
the generous helping.

618
00:20:59,100 --> 00:21:00,667
Geez.

619
00:21:00,667 --> 00:21:01,767
- [Mike] Uh, Dad, if you
could carve a little faster

620
00:21:01,767 --> 00:21:03,767
I'm kind of hungry.

621
00:21:03,767 --> 00:21:06,167
(grand orchestral music)

622
00:21:06,167 --> 00:21:08,000
- [Crow] Yes, chicken
sliced to the width

623
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:09,267
of one electron.

624
00:21:09,267 --> 00:21:11,500
- [Tom] We must be in
models portions, here.

625
00:21:13,700 --> 00:21:16,267
- [Mike] Miss Moss, and
for you, Miss Taylor.

626
00:21:18,267 --> 00:21:20,500
- [Crow] Yes, it's chicken,

627
00:21:20,500 --> 00:21:22,167
glorious American chicken sliced

628
00:21:22,167 --> 00:21:23,600
the American way.

629
00:21:23,600 --> 00:21:24,800
- [Mike] Carry it
in your wallet.

630
00:21:27,100 --> 00:21:29,667
- [Voiceover] But it's your
pocketbook that profits most

631
00:21:29,667 --> 00:21:32,033
when you send this
bird to market.

632
00:21:32,033 --> 00:21:33,700
- [Mike] We bring
you now to market.

633
00:21:33,700 --> 00:21:35,700
- [Voiceover] A unique
method of marketing chickens

634
00:21:35,700 --> 00:21:38,000
and eggs that's
grown in popularity

635
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:39,833
in recent years is the aucition.

636
00:21:39,833 --> 00:21:42,333
- [Tom] Such as
here, at Sutheby's.

637
00:21:42,333 --> 00:21:43,733
- [Voiceover] The first
one is said to have been

638
00:21:43,733 --> 00:21:46,267
the Hunterdon County at
Flemington, New Jersey.

639
00:21:46,267 --> 00:21:47,533
- [Mike] Oh, that's
an urban myth.

640
00:21:47,533 --> 00:21:48,500
- [Voiceover] But
the idea has spread

641
00:21:48,500 --> 00:21:49,767
to all parts of the country.

642
00:21:49,767 --> 00:21:52,667
- [Crow] The chicken
shortage of 1937.

643
00:21:52,667 --> 00:21:54,500
- [Voiceover] The farmers
bring their crates of eggs

644
00:21:54,500 --> 00:21:56,000
to market the day
before the auction.

645
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:57,800
- [Tom] Alternatively,
they may bring their eggs

646
00:21:57,800 --> 00:21:59,500
early the day of the auction.

647
00:22:00,933 --> 00:22:03,100
- [Voiceover] On auction
day, the buyers gather

648
00:22:03,100 --> 00:22:05,467
to purchase eggs and
poultry at prices

649
00:22:05,467 --> 00:22:06,767
opening arrived at.

650
00:22:06,767 --> 00:22:07,700
- [Auctioneer] Bid two, two.

651
00:22:07,700 --> 00:22:09,233
Two over there, two's the bid.

652
00:22:09,233 --> 00:22:10,367
It's amazing.

653
00:22:10,367 --> 00:22:11,300
- [Mike] You're adorable.

654
00:22:11,300 --> 00:22:12,133
- 17 a quarter and a half.

655
00:22:12,133 --> 00:22:13,200
It's 17 and a half.

656
00:22:13,200 --> 00:22:15,933
(yelling)

657
00:22:15,933 --> 00:22:16,867
- [Mike] Ross
Perot or something.

658
00:22:16,867 --> 00:22:18,067
- Three quarters the bid.

659
00:22:18,067 --> 00:22:20,033
Two, twos the bid,
now two and a quarter.

660
00:22:20,033 --> 00:22:21,000
- [Tom] He's drunk.

661
00:22:21,867 --> 00:22:23,800
- Here, 17's the bid!

662
00:22:23,800 --> 00:22:26,833
(speaking gibberish)

663
00:22:26,833 --> 00:22:28,033
(clapping)

664
00:22:28,033 --> 00:22:29,800
- One and a half, one
and a half, $1.75.

665
00:22:29,800 --> 00:22:31,633
- [Tom] Get your hand up.

666
00:22:31,633 --> 00:22:32,567
Buy a chicken.

667
00:22:32,567 --> 00:22:34,333
- [Mike] I don't want one.

668
00:22:34,333 --> 00:22:36,767
- [Voiceover] And after the
eggs and poultry are sold

669
00:22:36,767 --> 00:22:39,833
once again the motortruck
plays its big role.

670
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:42,167
- [Crow] Alright,
come on, sing, sing.

671
00:22:42,167 --> 00:22:43,967
Row, row, row,
you're not singing!

672
00:22:43,967 --> 00:22:46,500
- [Voiceover] Hundreds of
live chickens can be speeded

673
00:22:46,500 --> 00:22:47,800
on their way to
the dressing plant.

674
00:22:47,800 --> 00:22:49,633
- [Crow] The dressing
plant, sounds like fun.

675
00:22:49,633 --> 00:22:51,367
- [Tom] I want a new hat.

676
00:22:51,367 --> 00:22:53,333
(brassy orchestral music)

677
00:22:53,333 --> 00:22:55,300
- [Voiceover] One truck can
handle thousands of eggs

678
00:22:55,300 --> 00:22:57,467
and take them anywhere
there's a market.

679
00:22:57,467 --> 00:22:59,233
- [Mike] Even to
the Texaco station.

680
00:23:00,900 --> 00:23:04,067
- [Voiceover] Speed and
flexibility, that's what

681
00:23:04,067 --> 00:23:07,067
the industry must have
in it's transportation.

682
00:23:07,067 --> 00:23:08,067
- [Tom] You?

683
00:23:09,467 --> 00:23:11,800
- [Voiceover] And that's
exactly what the motortruck,

684
00:23:11,800 --> 00:23:13,200
fueled and lubricated with

685
00:23:13,200 --> 00:23:15,433
quality petroleum
products, offers.

686
00:23:16,833 --> 00:23:19,067
(snoring)

687
00:23:19,067 --> 00:23:21,100
- [Crow] Buddy, buddy, wake up.

688
00:23:22,200 --> 00:23:23,667
Hey, buddy.

689
00:23:23,667 --> 00:23:25,300
- [Voiceover] Ask any
poultryman and he'll tell you

690
00:23:25,300 --> 00:23:28,600
that the industry could not
flourish as it's doing today

691
00:23:28,600 --> 00:23:31,133
without the efficient
fleets of trucks

692
00:23:31,133 --> 00:23:33,333
that whisk baby chicks
to waiting farmers.

693
00:23:33,333 --> 00:23:34,500
- [Tom] To be killed.

694
00:23:34,500 --> 00:23:35,300
- [Voiceover] Rush
chickens to the grocer's

695
00:23:35,300 --> 00:23:36,100
and butcher's shelves.

696
00:23:36,100 --> 00:23:37,333
- [Tom] To be killed.

697
00:23:37,333 --> 00:23:38,867
- [Voiceover] And
speed fresh eggs

698
00:23:38,867 --> 00:23:40,100
to your breakfast table.

699
00:23:40,100 --> 00:23:41,700
- [Mike] Did America
really need to be sold

700
00:23:41,700 --> 00:23:43,800
on the automotive
industry at this point?

701
00:23:43,800 --> 00:23:44,800
- [Crow] There's no driver.

702
00:23:44,800 --> 00:23:46,533
The chickens are taking over!

703
00:23:46,533 --> 00:23:48,733
Woohoo.

704
00:23:48,733 --> 00:23:52,900
(squawking)
(old orchestral music)

705
00:23:58,867 --> 00:24:02,767
(creaking)
(bolts clanking)

706
00:24:02,767 --> 00:24:04,267
(beeping)
(engine whirring)

707
00:24:04,267 --> 00:24:06,600
(door sliding)
(chains clinking)

708
00:24:06,600 --> 00:24:09,700
(door slamming)
(metal scraping)

709
00:24:09,700 --> 00:24:11,200
(door closes)

710
00:24:11,200 --> 00:24:13,267
- Should I be
sitting astride you

711
00:24:13,267 --> 00:24:16,600
with my warm and nurturing
hindquarters or anything?

712
00:24:16,600 --> 00:24:18,500
- [Tom] No, no, I'm just fine.

713
00:24:18,500 --> 00:24:19,733
- How-de-do, Crow.

714
00:24:19,733 --> 00:24:20,667
Hey have you seen
Tom around anywhere?

715
00:24:20,667 --> 00:24:22,033
- [Tom] Hello, Mike,

716
00:24:22,033 --> 00:24:23,400
I'm in here!

717
00:24:23,400 --> 00:24:24,900
(laughing)

718
00:24:24,900 --> 00:24:26,133
- Hey, Tom, how are you
doing, you okay in there?

719
00:24:26,133 --> 00:24:27,500
- [Tom] Oh, more than
okay, Mike, I'm floating

720
00:24:27,500 --> 00:24:29,100
in a sea of warm
nutrients and I'm all set

721
00:24:29,100 --> 00:24:31,033
to begin my 21 day
gestation period.

722
00:24:31,033 --> 00:24:33,800
I want to experience the
miracle of chicken birth, Mike.

723
00:24:33,800 --> 00:24:35,500
- Yeah, plus,
there's a huge market

724
00:24:35,500 --> 00:24:36,900
for them egg-hatching movies.

725
00:24:36,900 --> 00:24:39,433
So we got Cambot
filming the whole thing.

726
00:24:39,433 --> 00:24:40,367
- [Tom] Yep, yep.

727
00:24:40,367 --> 00:24:41,600
- Yeah, that's, that's great.

728
00:24:41,600 --> 00:24:43,033
You know, it really is
beautiful, too, Tom,

729
00:24:43,033 --> 00:24:45,500
but I wish you'd told me
about it before you did this.

730
00:24:45,500 --> 00:24:46,700
- [Tom] Really, why?

731
00:24:46,700 --> 00:24:49,000
- Well, you're the
only one that, hello?

732
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:50,267
- [Tom] Yeah, go
on with your story.

733
00:24:50,267 --> 00:24:52,200
- Well, I was gonna say,
three weeks without you,

734
00:24:52,200 --> 00:24:53,967
you're the only one I
depend on for any kind

735
00:24:53,967 --> 00:24:55,433
of urbane conversation.

736
00:24:55,433 --> 00:24:56,733
- [Tom] Well, I
understand, Mike,

737
00:24:56,733 --> 00:24:58,200
but it's too late now.

738
00:24:58,200 --> 00:25:00,333
Just put me in a warm place,
soak me in some enzymes.

739
00:25:00,333 --> 00:25:01,933
- No, you know what, I
gotta get you out of there.

740
00:25:01,933 --> 00:25:03,467
I can't live without you.

741
00:25:03,467 --> 00:25:04,867
- [Tom] No, no, Mike, Mike,
I'm not ready to hatch yet.

742
