twiddling", Commissioner.

1228
00:41:09,967 --> 00:41:11,467
- [Mike] Rochester.

1229
00:41:11,467 --> 00:41:12,267
- [Parkington] Then just what
would you call it, Captain?

1230
00:41:12,267 --> 00:41:13,667
- [Tom] Thumb wrestling.

1231
00:41:13,667 --> 00:41:14,933
- His honor, the mayor,
would like to know.

1232
00:41:14,933 --> 00:41:16,600
- Now if you gentlemen
will please sit down,

1233
00:41:16,600 --> 00:41:18,333
I'll try to bring you up
to date on the creeper.

1234
00:41:18,333 --> 00:41:20,067
- We are up to date
on the creeper.

1235
00:41:20,067 --> 00:41:22,000
We know that you
haven't arrested him,

1236
00:41:22,000 --> 00:41:23,733
and his honor, the mayor,
delivered an ultimatim

1237
00:41:23,733 --> 00:41:25,700
to Commissioner
Salisbury this morning.

1238
00:41:25,700 --> 00:41:28,400
He demanded action
within 24 hours.

1239
00:41:28,400 --> 00:41:30,300
If none is forthcoming,
he will ask

1240
00:41:30,300 --> 00:41:31,700
for the commissioner's
resignation.

1241
00:41:31,700 --> 00:41:33,467
- And I'm telling you,
Donelly, if you don't

1242
00:41:33,467 --> 00:41:35,233
turn up something to
quiet public opinion

1243
00:41:35,233 --> 00:41:38,100
before tomorrow, your job
won't be worth a pumpernickel.

1244
00:41:38,100 --> 00:41:39,300
- [Crow] Get his puffer!

1245
00:41:39,300 --> 00:41:40,500
- Exactly what I was
telling Lieutenant Gates

1246
00:41:40,500 --> 00:41:43,067
when you came in,
wasn't I, Lieutenant?

1247
00:41:43,067 --> 00:41:46,100
- Yes, sir, you were,
and I'll pass the word

1248
00:41:46,100 --> 00:41:48,100
right on down the
line until every man

1249
00:41:48,100 --> 00:41:50,200
in the department knows exactly
where this thing stands.

1250
00:41:50,200 --> 00:41:52,333
You can depend on me, Gentemen.

1251
00:41:52,333 --> 00:41:53,300
- Very well.

1252
00:41:53,300 --> 00:41:54,500
Shall we go, Commissioner?

1253
00:41:55,967 --> 00:41:58,433
- [Crow and Tom] We
represent the Lollipop Guild,

1254
00:41:58,433 --> 00:42:00,700
the Lollipop Guild.

1255
00:42:00,700 --> 00:42:01,900
- How'd I do?

1256
00:42:01,900 --> 00:42:03,167
- Great, we're
still a good team.

1257
00:42:03,167 --> 00:42:04,133
- [Tom] That was a
long, dull scene.

1258
00:42:04,133 --> 00:42:05,467
(phone rings)
Congratulations.

1259
00:42:05,467 --> 00:42:07,967
- Homicide, Captain
Donelly speaking.

1260
00:42:07,967 --> 00:42:11,067
- [Mike] No, you're
looking for armed robbery.

1261
00:42:11,067 --> 00:42:13,833
- Okay, okay,
we'll look into it.

1262
00:42:13,833 --> 00:42:15,067
(phone clicks)

1263
00:42:15,067 --> 00:42:15,733
Everybody in town's
got creeper-itis.

1264
00:42:15,733 --> 00:42:16,967
- Who was it?

1265
00:42:16,967 --> 00:42:18,267
- Some grocer down
near the waterfront

1266
00:42:18,267 --> 00:42:19,633
thinks the creeper
might have knocked off

1267
00:42:19,633 --> 00:42:20,467
his delivery boy.

1268
00:42:20,467 --> 00:42:21,400
- [Crow] So?

1269
00:42:21,400 --> 00:42:23,433
Creeper, creeper, creeper.

1270
00:42:23,433 --> 00:42:25,867
- We just have time to go
down there before lunch.

1271
00:42:25,867 --> 00:42:29,300
(upbeat synth pop music)

1272
00:42:31,800 --> 00:42:33,267
- Whoa, whoa, whoa,
how long you gonna be

1273
00:42:33,267 --> 00:42:34,867
on that phone, there,
Tex, because I'm expecting

1274
00:42:34,867 --> 00:42:36,533
an important phone call
from Sheri, my realtor,

1275
00:42:36,533 --> 00:42:38,100
about the duplex and all.

1276
00:42:38,100 --> 00:42:39,400
- Yeah, that's great,
but listen, Tom,

1277
00:42:39,400 --> 00:42:41,400
I had the idea, hey,
we have a phone.

1278
00:42:41,400 --> 00:42:44,400
Why not call someone like
my old girlfriend, Karla,

1279
00:42:44,400 --> 00:42:45,800
and she could help us
get down from here.

1280
00:42:45,800 --> 00:42:47,433
- Oh, that's a cute
idea, too, Mike,

1281
00:42:47,433 --> 00:42:49,100
but keep it around
30 seconds, will you,

1282
00:42:49,100 --> 00:42:50,867
because Sheri's probably
trying to get a hold of me.

1283
00:42:50,867 --> 00:42:52,033
(phone dialing)
- Yeah, whatever, it's fine.

1284
00:42:52,033 --> 00:42:52,867
- [Voiceover] Hello?

1285
00:42:52,867 --> 00:42:54,100
- Oh, hi, Karla.

1286
00:42:54,100 --> 00:42:56,267
Hey it's me, Mike Nelson.

1287
00:42:56,267 --> 00:42:57,100
- [Voiceover] Mike!

1288
00:42:57,100 --> 00:42:58,167
Hi!

1289
00:42:58,167 --> 00:42:59,367
How are you?

1290
00:42:59,367 --> 00:43:00,933
- I'm good.

1291
00:43:00,933 --> 00:43:02,200
Hey, listen very carefully,
this is gonna sound weird,

1292
00:43:02,200 --> 00:43:03,967
but I'm trapped up in space--

1293
00:43:03,967 --> 00:43:05,467
- [Voiceover] Oh, hang on, Mike,

1294
00:43:05,467 --> 00:43:07,400
my little boy, Matthew,
wants to say "hi" to you.

1295
00:43:07,400 --> 00:43:08,400
- Well, no, this is kind of--

1296
00:43:08,400 --> 00:43:09,733
- [Voiceover] Go ahead, Matthew.

1297
00:43:09,733 --> 00:43:10,567
(heavy breathing)

1298
00:43:10,567 --> 00:43:11,400
Say "hi" to Mike.

1299
00:43:11,400 --> 00:43:12,600
- Hi, Matt.

1300
00:43:12,600 --> 00:43:13,933
- [Voiceover] Say, "Hi, Mike."

1301
00:43:13,933 --> 00:43:14,867
- Hi.

1302
00:43:14,867 --> 00:43:15,800
- [Voiceover] Say, "Hi, Mike."

1303
00:43:15,800 --> 00:43:16,667
- Hi.

1304
00:43:16,667 --> 00:43:18,100
- [Voiceover] Hi.

1305
00:43:18,100 --> 00:43:21,400
- Hi, could you give the
phone back now, please, Matt?

1306
00:43:21,400 --> 00:43:24,300
(high voice babbling)

1307
00:43:24,300 --> 00:43:26,700
That's great, Matt,
that's terrific,

1308
00:43:26,700 --> 00:43:28,167
sounds like fun.

1309
00:43:28,167 --> 00:43:29,833
Hey, could you give the
phone back to your mom, now?

1310
00:43:29,833 --> 00:43:31,967
(high voice babbling)

1311
00:43:31,967 --> 00:43:32,900
Great, uh-huh.

1312
00:43:32,900 --> 00:43:34,100
Give the phone back, Matt!

1313
00:43:34,100 --> 00:43:35,100
- Mike, let me
take care of this,

1314
00:43:35,100 --> 00:43:36,200
let me take care of this.

1315
00:43:36,200 --> 00:43:37,100
Matt, can you hear me?

1316
00:43:37,100 --> 00:43:38,300
- [Voiceover] Yeah.

1317
00:43:38,300 --> 00:43:39,767
- Listen, Son,
hang up the phone.

1318
00:43:39,767 --> 00:43:40,567
I'm trying to get a hold
of my realtor, Sheri, okay?

1319
00:43:40,567 --> 00:43:41,500
- [Voiceover] Okay, bye.

1320
00:43:41,500 --> 00:43:42,933
- No, don't hang up, Matt!

1321
00:43:42,933 --> 00:43:43,867
(dial tone droning)
Now look what you've done!

1322
00:43:43,867 --> 00:43:44,767
This was our chance to get down.

1323
00:43:44,767 --> 00:43:45,800
(alarm buzzing)

1324
00:43:45,800 --> 00:43:47,033
- What'd I do?

1325
00:43:47,033 --> 00:43:48,100
It's only my realtor,
for crying out loud.

1326
00:43:48,100 --> 00:43:49,300
(engine whirring)

1327
00:43:49,300 --> 00:43:51,767
(metal scraping)

1328
00:43:51,767 --> 00:43:53,233
(door slams)

1329
00:43:53,233 --> 00:43:54,333
(chains clinking)
(liquid dripping)

1330
00:43:54,333 --> 00:43:56,533
(door sliding)
(beeping)

1331
00:43:56,533 --> 00:43:59,900
(bolts clanking)

1332
00:43:59,900 --> 00:44:01,833
- [Crow] Creeper, creeper.

1333
00:44:03,300 --> 00:44:05,500
- [Tom] Oh, I do
the best stir fry.

1334
00:44:05,500 --> 00:44:06,633
You're in for a treat, my little

1335
00:44:06,633 --> 00:44:07,633
delivery-boy friend.

1336
00:44:07,633 --> 00:44:09,333
- [Mike] Hey, wake up, sunshine.

1337
00:44:09,333 --> 00:44:11,600
- [Crow] Oh, he used the
last set of clean sheets.

1338
00:44:12,933 --> 00:44:15,500
(ominous orchestral music)
(engine rumbling)

1339
00:44:15,500 --> 00:44:16,800
- [Tom] Oh, no, the apaches.

1340
00:44:17,800 --> 00:44:19,033
(engine rumbling)

1341
00:44:19,033 --> 00:44:19,700
- [Mike] This is
a noisy apartment.

1342
00:44:20,867 --> 00:44:22,500
- [Lieutenant] Here's
number 23, Donelly.

1343
00:44:22,500 --> 00:44:24,833
- [Crow] Be quiet, we
have to sneak up on him.

1344
00:44:26,033 --> 00:44:27,267
(water lapping)

1345
00:44:27,267 --> 00:44:29,733
- [Tom] So, I got time
to snag a sturgeon?

1346
00:44:29,733 --> 00:44:30,967
- [Crow] Come on, here, boy.

1347
00:44:30,967 --> 00:44:32,033
Heel.

1348
00:44:32,033 --> 00:44:35,733
(ominous orchestral music)

1349
00:44:35,733 --> 00:44:37,500
- [Mike] Oh, I forgot about
the realtor coming over.

1350
00:44:37,500 --> 00:44:39,400
- [Crow] I'll just push
the body under the rug,

1351
00:44:39,400 --> 00:44:40,867
and give it a lick and promise.

1352
00:44:40,867 --> 00:44:43,000
- [Tom] Hate to leave
my boards behind.

1353
00:44:44,067 --> 00:44:46,367
(footsteps)

1354
00:44:46,367 --> 00:44:48,700
- [Mike] I door-knocked out
here for Wellstone, once.

1355
00:44:48,700 --> 00:44:50,667
- [Crow] Hey, it's Katie May.

1356
00:44:50,667 --> 00:44:52,700
(door handle rattling)

1357
00:44:52,700 --> 00:44:54,200
- [Tom] Hello?

1358
00:44:54,200 --> 00:44:55,433
(doorknob rattling)

1359
00:44:55,433 --> 00:44:57,467
- [Donelly] Come on,
open up in there.

1360
00:44:57,467 --> 00:44:59,533
- [Crow] Oh no.

1361
00:44:59,533 --> 00:45:01,667
(door breaking)

1362
00:45:01,667 --> 00:45:02,667
- [Mike] Beep, beep.

1363
00:45:02,667 --> 00:45:05,400
Alert, intruder,
alert, intruder.

1364
00:45:05,400 --> 00:45:08,767
- [Crow] Hmm, what a
really good plant house.

1365
00:45:10,133 --> 00:45:11,367
- [Tom] The crawl
space is a little wet,

1366
00:45:11,367 --> 00:45:12,567
but there's a sump pump.

1367
00:45:15,900 --> 00:45:17,700
- [Mike] I hate these
shared bathrooms.

1368
00:45:18,567 --> 00:45:21,100
(ominous orchestral music)

1369
00:45:21,100 --> 00:45:22,533
- Look.

1370
00:45:22,533 --> 00:45:23,367
- [Crow] Can it wait?

1371
00:45:23,367 --> 00:45:24,567
I'm fixing eggs.

1372
00:45:26,433 --> 00:45:29,100
- [Crow] Does this
body mean anything?

1373
00:45:29,100 --> 00:45:31,433
(footsteps)

1374
00:45:32,267 --> 00:45:33,367
- [Mike] Okay, I looked.

1375
00:45:33,367 --> 00:45:34,200
Happy?

1376
00:45:34,200 --> 00:45:35,300
- The creeper again.

1377
00:45:35,300 --> 00:45:36,133
- Yep.

1378
00:45:37,833 --> 00:45:39,000
From the looks of
that coffee pot,

1379
00:45:39,000 --> 00:45:40,233
he can't be far away.

1380
00:45:40,233 --> 00:45:41,967
- [Tom] He never
leaves half a pot.

1381
00:45:41,967 --> 00:45:43,233
- Round up some men and start
combing the neighborhood

1382
00:45:43,233 --> 00:45:44,067
and I'll have a
look around here.

1383
00:45:44,067 --> 00:45:44,900
- Okay.

1384
00:45:46,300 --> 00:45:48,133
- [Crow] Where
are those filters?

1385
00:45:48,133 --> 00:45:49,633
- [Mike] Oh, god,
the creepers bedpan.

1386
00:45:49,633 --> 00:45:51,700
- [Tom and Crow] Oh, eww.

1387
00:45:52,833 --> 00:45:54,867
(footsteps)

1388
00:45:54,867 --> 00:45:55,800
- [Tom] Well, that does it.

1389
00:45:55,800 --> 00:45:56,533
My investigation's complete.

1390
00:45:59,633 --> 00:46:01,267
- [Crow] Say, you're
one good-looking man.

1391
00:46:01,267 --> 00:46:02,267
Who loves you, baby?

1392
00:46:02,267 --> 00:46:03,100
Oh, a zit there.

1393
00:46:04,533 --> 00:46:05,367
(door opens)

1394
00:46:05,367 --> 00:46:07,000
- [Mike] Hi, guy.

1395
00:46:07,000 --> 00:46:09,233
- [Tom] Look at all
these Lanecome products.

1396
00:46:09,233 --> 00:46:11,967
(paper rustling)

1397
00:46:13,300 --> 00:46:15,867
- [Crow] These three saw
Popular Trio, recently.

1398
00:46:17,267 --> 00:46:19,667
- [Tom] Popular Trio
still under investigation.

1399
00:46:21,133 --> 00:46:22,933
- [Mike] Oh, man, yeah,
they were popular.

1400
00:46:22,933 --> 00:46:25,500
So full of life, it was
great to be around them.

1401
00:46:25,500 --> 00:46:27,333
Well, I'll look for more clues.

1402
00:46:29,333 --> 00:46:31,633
- [Crow] Oh, he sure
is into coupons.

1403
00:46:31,633 --> 00:46:34,067
- [Mike] Look at this, he put
applesauce in his meatloaf.

1404
00:46:34,067 --> 00:46:35,233
That's a terrific idea.

1405
00:46:36,600 --> 00:46:38,467
- [Tom] Yep, sure
is creeping, there.

1406
00:46:40,433 --> 00:46:42,033
- [Crow] Oh, damn,
where's that northern?

1407
00:46:42,033 --> 00:46:43,767
I had it chained up right here.

1408
00:46:44,733 --> 00:46:46,000
(footsteps)

1409
00:46:46,000 --> 00:46:47,433
- [Mike] No, I won't
talk to you anymore.

1410
00:46:49,633 --> 00:46:50,833
(water swishing)

1411
00:46:50,833 --> 00:46:52,100
- [Tom] Well, looks
like we combed the area.

1412
00:46:53,067 --> 00:46:55,900
(laughing)

1413
00:46:55,900 --> 00:46:57,333
- I'm going to call
headquarters for more men.

1414
00:46:57,333 --> 00:46:59,333
We'll give the whole district
a house to house canvas.

1415
00:46:59,333 --> 00:47:01,300
- [Crow] That'll take a
very large canvas, sir.

1416
00:47:01,300 --> 00:47:03,767
- [Tom] Say, anybody know
where the AJ Fiske and Son

1417
00:47:03,767 --> 00:47:05,200
Sausage Company is?

1418
00:47:07,000 --> 00:47:09,867
And who is walking on my bridge?

1419
00:47:09,867 --> 00:47:11,000
(gasp)

1420
00:47:11,000 --> 00:47:12,233
- [Tom] Boss delighted.

1421
00:47:12,233 --> 00:47:14,300
- [Crow] "Where is olive
loaf?", people demand.

1422
00:47:16,333 --> 00:47:20,200
(scary orchestral music)

1423
00:47:20,200 --> 00:47:21,700
- [Mike] Boy, you know,
murder clues may lead

1424
00:47:21,700 --> 00:47:22,767
to creeper.

1425
00:47:22,767 --> 00:47:26,033
(ominous orchestral music)

1426
00:47:26,033 --> 00:47:29,300
- [Tom] Meanwhile, the thin
man arrives at the White House,

1427
00:47:29,300 --> 00:47:30,133
and his brother.

1428
00:47:34,367 --> 00:47:35,633
- [Crow] Nice knockers, huh?

1429
00:47:35,633 --> 00:47:38,267
- [Mike] Love that joke.
(laughing)

1430
00:47:39,133 --> 00:47:40,467
(knocking)

1431
00:47:40,467 --> 00:47:41,500
- [Crow] Trick or
treat for UNICEF.

1432
00:47:41,500 --> 00:47:42,333
(door opens)

1433
00:47:42,333 --> 00:47:43,167
- Good evening.

1434
00:47:43,167 --> 00:47:44,100
- Good evening.

1435
00:47:44,100 --> 00:47:44,833
- Are you Mr. Clifford Scott?

1436
00:47:44,833 --> 00:47:46,067
- Yes.

1437
00:47:46,067 --> 00:47:46,900
- Captain Donelly,
Homicide Burrow,

1438
00:47:46,900 --> 00:47:48,067
and Lieutenant Gates.

1439
00:47:48,067 --> 00:47:49,000
Mind if we come in?

1440
00:47:49,000 --> 00:47:50,133
- Not at all, come right in.

1441
00:47:50,133 --> 00:47:51,500
- Thank you.

1442
00:47:51,500 --> 00:47:52,333
- [Mike] We were
wondering why you weren't

1443
00:47:52,333 --> 00:47:53,567
wearing a hat.

1444
00:47:53,567 --> 00:47:54,500
(door closes)

1445
00:47:54,500 --> 00:47:55,733
- [Tom] Tom Dewey, at home.

1446
00:47:55,733 --> 00:47:57,233
- What can I do
for you gentlemen?

1447
00:47:57,233 --> 00:47:58,333
- I believe you can give us
some important information

1448
00:47:58,333 --> 00:47:59,567
if you will.

1449
00:47:59,567 --> 00:48:00,867
- I don't know what
this is all about

1450
00:48:00,867 --> 00:48:02,267
but I'll be glad to help
in any way that I can.

1451
00:48:02,267 --> 00:48:03,267
Shall we go into
the living room?

1452
00:48:03,267 --> 00:48:04,467
- Thank you.

1453
00:48:04,467 --> 00:48:06,300
- [Tom] It's just
this way about a mile.

1454
00:48:06,300 --> 00:48:07,233
- What is it, Clifford?

1455
00:48:07,233 --> 00:48:08,467
- I don't know yet, dear.

1456
00:48:08,467 --> 00:48:09,833
These gentlemen are from
the police department.

1457
00:48:09,833 --> 00:48:11,233
They say they'd like to
ask me some questions.

1458
00:48:11,233 --> 00:48:12,867
Captain Donelly,
Lieutenant Gates, my wife.

1459
00:48:12,867 --> 00:48:14,100
- How do you do?

1460
00:48:14,100 --> 00:48:15,267
- You can help us,
too, Mrs. Scott.

1461
00:48:15,267 --> 00:48:16,100
- I?

1462
00:48:16,100 --> 00:48:17,300
- Yes.

1463
00:48:17,300 --> 00:48:18,500
I take it you've read
about the murders

1464
00:48:18,500 --> 00:48:19,933
committed by a character
the newspapers call

1465
00:48:19,933 --> 00:48:20,800
"the creeper"?

1466
00:48:20,800 --> 00:48:21,967
- Why, yes.

1467
00:48:21,967 --> 00:48:23,733
- We stumbled onto
something this morning.

1468
00:48:23,733 --> 00:48:24,967
- [Crow] We stubbed our toe.

1469
00:48:24,967 --> 00:48:25,867
- This leads me
to believe you can

1470
00:48:25,867 --> 00:48:27,100
help us identify him.

1471
00:48:27,100 --> 00:48:28,833
- You mean it's someone we know?

1472
00:48:28,833 --> 00:48:30,067
- I think so.

1473
00:48:30,067 --> 00:48:32,267
What was your class
at Hampton University?

1474
00:48:32,267 --> 00:48:33,733
- 1930.

1475
00:48:33,733 --> 00:48:35,367
- You were in that class,
too, weren't you, Mrs. Scott?

1476
00:48:35,367 --> 00:48:36,767
- Yes.

1477
00:48:36,767 --> 00:48:38,667
- And you had a classmate
named Hal Moffat?

1478
00:48:38,667 --> 00:48:39,600
Remember him?

1479
00:48:39,600 --> 00:48:40,567
- He was a good friend of ours.

1480
00:48:40,567 --> 00:48:42,033
- [Mike] He sat on a tuffet.

1481
00:48:42,033 --> 00:48:44,733
- We have an idea that
Hal Moffat is the creeper.

1482
00:48:44,733 --> 00:48:45,567
- Hal?

1483
00:48:45,567 --> 00:48:47,300
- No, he couldn't be.

1484
00:48:47,300 --> 00:48:49,000
- We found this in
his shack, among some

1485
00:48:49,000 --> 00:48:50,467
of this other things.

1486
00:48:50,467 --> 00:48:52,533
Apparently, he'd been
keeping it for some time.

1487
00:48:52,533 --> 00:48:53,467
- [Tom] Let me see here.

1488
00:48:53,467 --> 00:48:54,900
I'm Hal Moffet, I'm the creeper.

1489
00:48:54,900 --> 00:48:55,967
What do you know?

1490
00:48:55,967 --> 00:48:57,167
(paper rustling)

1491
00:48:57,167 --> 00:48:58,100
- [Clifford] I remember
when this was taken.

1492
00:48:58,100 --> 00:48:59,567
Was right after a football game.

1493
00:48:59,567 --> 00:49:03,233
- Yes, it was the day Hal almost
won the game single-handed.

1494
00:49:03,233 --> 00:49:06,000
- Two people from that college
were killed by the creeper.

1495
00:49:06,000 --> 00:49:09,533
Professor Cushman and a
woman named Joan Bemis.

1496
00:49:09,533 --> 00:49:10,867
- We knew Joan quite well.

1497
00:49:10,867 --> 00:49:12,467
- I thought you might of.

1498
00:49:12,467 --> 00:49:14,267
When did you last
see Hal Moffat?

1499
00:49:14,267 --> 00:49:15,500
- [Mike] He was
eating curds and whey.

1500
00:49:15,500 --> 00:49:16,600
- We haven't seen him for years.

1501
00:49:16,600 --> 00:49:18,600
- No, he just disappeared.

1502
00:49:18,600 --> 00:49:19,867
- [Donelly] Disappeared?

1503
00:49:19,867 --> 00:49:20,900
- Well, none of us
has seen him since

1504
00:49:20,900 --> 00:49:22,767
his last year in college.

1505
00:49:22,767 --> 00:49:24,900
You see, something
happened to Hal.

1506
00:49:24,900 --> 00:49:25,733
- [Donelly] What?

1507
00:49:25,733 --> 00:49:26,967
- [Crow] He graduated.

1508
00:49:26,967 --> 00:49:28,300
- He was probably
our best friend.

1509
00:49:28,300 --> 00:49:29,633
- [Mike] If I'm thinking
of the right guy.

1510
00:49:29,633 --> 00:49:31,133
- His last year at
Hampton, he was captain

1511
00:49:31,133 --> 00:49:32,433
of the football team.

1512
00:49:32,433 --> 00:49:33,867
- [Tom] Here we go.

1513
00:49:33,867 --> 00:49:35,400
- [Clifford] In the
final game of the season,

1514
00:49:35,400 --> 00:49:36,900
we were playing Rensler for
the Compton's championship.

1515
00:49:36,900 --> 00:49:39,833
Sports writers picked Hal
for All-American honors.

1516
00:49:39,833 --> 00:49:42,000
If they had any doubt about
it, the game he played

1517
00:49:42,000 --> 00:49:43,333
against Princeton
won their votes.

1518
00:49:43,333 --> 00:49:44,800
- [Crow] Harpo!

1519
00:49:44,800 --> 00:49:46,567
- [Clifford] Hal had a quick
temper, but it certainly

1520
00:49:46,567 --> 00:49:47,933
paid off on the football field.

1521
00:49:47,933 --> 00:49:49,200
- [Crow] Sieg, Heil!

1522
00:49:49,200 --> 00:49:50,433
- [Clifford] He was
all over the place,

1523
00:49:50,433 --> 00:49:51,733
blocking, backing up his
line, passing so much

1524
00:49:51,733 --> 00:49:53,867
we were dizzy, and up
to the fourth quarter,

1525
00:49:53,867 --> 00:49:55,733
had scored three touchdowns
and brilliant broken

1526
00:49:55,733 --> 00:49:56,967
field runs.

1527
00:49:56,967 --> 00:49:58,500
- [Tom] 14 RBIs
and three assists.

1528
00:49:58,500 --> 00:50:00,500
- [Clifford] Three times he
converted for extra points.

1529
00:50:00,500 --> 00:50:04,133
Score stood: Hampton,
21, Rensler, seven.

1530
00:50:04,133 --> 00:50:05,400
- [Mike] Okay,
great, right there.

1531
00:50:05,400 --> 00:50:07,067
Don't move it, no,
you moved it, no.

1532
00:50:07,067 --> 00:50:08,300
(crowd cheering)

1533
00:50:08,300 --> 00:50:09,533
- [Crow] Ah, hang on,
I'm having a private

1534
00:50:09,533 --> 00:50:10,967
flashback right now,
I'll get back to you.

1535
00:50:12,167 --> 00:50:15,233
(whistling)
(yelling)

1536
00:50:15,233 --> 00:50:17,500
- [Clifford] Hal's temper
made him a lot of enemies,

1537
00:50:17,500 --> 00:50:19,567
but this year, everyone
was his friend.

1538
00:50:19,567 --> 00:50:21,633
He was one of the best
players Hampton ever produced

1539
00:50:21,633 --> 00:50:23,567
and we were proud of him.

1540
00:50:23,567 --> 00:50:24,933
- [Tom] Ah, sir,
this is really neat,

1541
00:50:24,933 --> 00:50:25,767
(gun firing)
but could we get back to

1542
00:50:25,767 --> 00:50:26,900
the murder case?

1543
00:50:26,900 --> 00:50:28,133
(crowd cheering)

1544
00:50:28,133 --> 00:50:29,367
- [Clifford] Hal and I
were very close friends,

1545
00:50:29,367 --> 00:50:30,867
and there was only
one catch to it.

1546
00:50:30,867 --> 00:50:33,067
We were both in
love with Virginia,

1547
00:50:33,067 --> 00:50:35,167
and to complicate
matters, Joan Bemis

1548
00:50:35,167 --> 00:50:36,300
was madly in love with Hal.

1549
00:50:36,300 --> 00:50:37,833
- [Crow] A football.

1550
00:50:37,833 --> 00:50:39,600
- [Clifford] We were going
to celebrate that night,

1551
00:50:39,600 --> 00:50:40,633
the four of us.

1552
00:50:40,633 --> 00:50:41,933
- [Mike] We each
had patty melts.

1553
00:50:41,933 --> 00:50:43,400
- [Clifford] I had a
date with Virginia,

1554
00:50:43,400 --> 00:50:45,600
but Hal did a little conniving.

1555
00:50:45,600 --> 00:50:46,900
He picked her up in
his car and didn't

1556
00:50:46,900 --> 00:50:48,800
arrive until the
evening was half over,

1557
00:50:48,800 --> 00:50:50,667
leaving Joan and me
to amuse ourselves

1558
00:50:50,667 --> 00:50:52,833
by staring at each
other across the table.

1559
00:50:52,833 --> 00:50:54,067
- [Tom] Ridiculous, huh?

1560
00:50:54,067 --> 00:50:54,900
- [Clifford] Of course,
he had a story ready

1561
00:50:54,900 --> 00:50:56,433
when they did get there.

1562
00:50:56,433 --> 00:50:58,100
He always had a story,

1563
00:50:58,100 --> 00:51:01,033
and knowing his temper,
I seldom challenged him.

1564
00:51:01,033 --> 00:51:02,300
- Hi.

1565
00:51:02,300 --> 00:51:03,500
- [Crow] Well, what
do you mean by that?

1566
00:51:03,500 --> 00:51:04,733
- I'm sorry we're
late, folks, but I had

1567
00:51:04,733 --> 00:51:05,600
some car trouble
and couldn't seem

1568
00:51:05,600 --> 00:51:07,133
to find out what it was.

1569
00:51:07,133 --> 00:51:09,400
All of a sudden, it
started, just like that.

1570
00:51:09,400 --> 00:51:10,633
You know how it
is, Cliff, old boy.

1571
00:51:10,633 --> 00:51:13,333
- [Clifford] Hal did
the scoring again,

1572
00:51:13,333 --> 00:51:15,667
just like he did on the
field that afternoon.

1573
00:51:15,667 --> 00:51:17,267
- [Crow] So tell me
about this cat poster,

1574
00:51:17,267 --> 00:51:19,567
apparently, the cat's
hanging on a stick.

1575
00:51:19,567 --> 00:51:20,800
- [Mike] Jag off, jerk.

1576
00:51:20,800 --> 00:51:22,600
- [Clifford] Hal and
I were roommates.

1577
00:51:22,600 --> 00:51:24,233
He was the athlete
and I was a scholar.

1578
00:51:24,233 --> 00:51:25,767
- [Crow] Let's
make lots of money.

1579
00:51:25,767 --> 00:51:27,767
- [Clifford] I used to help
him a lot with his studies.

1580
00:51:27,767 --> 00:51:30,067
It's an old college custom,
keeping football stars

1581
00:51:30,067 --> 00:51:31,800
eligible to play.

1582
00:51:31,800 --> 00:51:33,167
It wasn't because Hal was dumb.

1583
00:51:33,167 --> 00:51:34,633
- [Tom] But he was.

1584
00:51:34,633 --> 00:51:36,367
- [Clifford] He was just
to impatient to study hard.

1585
00:51:36,367 --> 00:51:38,400
On the football, he
got action quick,

1586
00:51:38,400 --> 00:51:40,400
but you can't stiff-arm
your way through a flock

1587
00:51:40,400 --> 00:51:42,200
of chemistry problems.

1588
00:51:42,200 --> 00:51:44,000
- Yep, I got a very
heavy date with Virginia

1589
00:51:44,000 --> 00:51:45,933
right after chemistry
class, and boy,

1590
00:51:45,933 --> 00:51:47,133
have I got plans.

1591
00:51:47,133 --> 00:51:48,400
- [Tom] Did I ask?

1592
00:51:48,400 --> 00:51:49,533
- [Clifford] Hal
did some boasting,

1593
00:51:49,533 --> 00:51:51,100
and I did some thinking.

1594
00:51:51,100 --> 00:51:52,333
Then he made the
mistake of asking me

1595
00:51:52,333 --> 00:51:54,467
to check his chemistry answers.

1596
00:51:54,467 --> 00:51:55,567
I saw a chance to
keep him from having

1597
00:51:55,567 --> 00:51:57,700
that date with Virginia.

1598
00:51:57,700 --> 00:52:00,767
His answers were right,
but I fixed that.

1599
00:52:00,767 --> 00:52:02,933
I gave him a set of
wrong ones, and he

1600
00:52:02,933 --> 00:52:04,467
memorized them very
carefully for the

1601
00:52:04,467 --> 00:52:05,767
verbal quiz scheduled
for the next day.

1602
00:52:05,767 --> 00:52:07,800
- [Mike] So you're
a big fat jerk.

1603
00:52:07,800 --> 00:52:10,033
- Hydrochloric
acid, sulfuric acid,

1604
00:52:10,033 --> 00:52:13,500
and nitric acid are
all catalytic agents.

1605
00:52:13,500 --> 00:52:14,433
- Very good, Mr. Scott.

1606
00:52:14,433 --> 00:52:16,067
- [Tom] But I didn't ask.

1607
00:52:16,067 --> 00:52:17,000
- Mr. Moffat.

1608
00:52:17,000 --> 00:52:18,533
- [Crow] Sit on my tuffet.

1609
00:52:18,533 --> 00:52:19,933
(desk clunks)

1610
00:52:19,933 --> 00:52:22,133
- Sulfuric acid, when combined
with H four and seven,

1611
00:52:22,133 --> 00:52:24,833
will form a solution
of hydrochloric acid.

1612
00:52:24,833 --> 00:52:26,367
- [Mike] Mr. Moffat,
I have never spanked

1613
00:52:26,367 --> 00:52:29,033
a senior before until now.

1614
00:52:29,033 --> 00:52:31,800
- You better remain
after class, Mr. Moffat.

1615
00:52:31,800 --> 00:52:34,533
You seem to have
everything wrong today.

1616
00:52:34,533 --> 00:52:35,767
Class dismissed.

1617
00:52:35,767 --> 00:52:37,533
- [Tom] He'll never suspect me.

1618
00:52:37,533 --> 00:52:39,867
- [Clifford] Hal realized
that I'd crossed him up.

1619
00:52:39,867 --> 00:52:41,700
He was accustomed
to being a winner,

1620
00:52:41,700 --> 00:52:43,133
never a loser.

1621
00:52:43,133 --> 00:52:44,667
He was plenty angry
because I'd outsmarted him

1622
00:52:44,667 --> 00:52:46,333
and he showed it.

1623
00:52:46,333 --> 00:52:48,600
- [Crow] I'm gonna grow
an ugly face and kill you.

1624
00:52:49,667 --> 00:52:51,000
(ominous orchestral music)

1625
00:52:51,000 --> 00:52:52,533
- [Clifford] Professor
Cushman put Hal to work

1626
00:52:52,533 --> 00:52:54,133
on a difficult experiment
that would keep him busy

1627
00:52:54,133 --> 00:52:56,033
the rest of the afternoon.

1628
00:52:56,033 --> 00:52:58,100
And just to needle
him, I walked Virginia

1629
00:52:58,100 --> 00:52:59,300
past the laboratory window.

1630
00:52:59,300 --> 00:53:00,567
- [Mike] Oh, you're
a good friend.

1631
00:53:00,567 --> 00:53:01,800
- [Clifford] The look
he gave us told me

1632
00:53:01,800 --> 00:53:03,433
I'd better get away
from there quick.

1633
00:53:03,433 --> 00:53:04,500
You could never
tell what he'd do

1634
00:53:04,500 --> 00:53:05,767
when his temper flared.

1635
00:53:05,767 --> 00:53:06,800
- [Crow] Come on, let's
go throw cigarette butts

1636
00:53:06,800 --> 00:53:08,200
at the monkeys.

1637
00:53:08,200 --> 00:53:10,333
(orchestral swell)

1638
00:53:10,333 --> 00:53:11,600
(glass shattering)

1639
00:53:11,600 --> 00:53:12,533
- [Crow] Ha!

1640
00:53:12,533 --> 00:53:14,367
- [Mike] Out, vile jelly.

1641
00:53:14,367 --> 00:53:15,233
- [Tom] Am I okay?

1642
00:53:15,233 --> 00:53:17,500
Yeah, I think I am.

1643
00:53:17,500 --> 00:53:18,967
- [Clifford] This time,
it brought tragedy.

1644
00:53:18,967 --> 00:53:20,167
- [Crow] Do you
want the lab notes?

1645
00:53:20,167 --> 00:53:21,733
- [Clifford] We visited
him in the hospital,

1646
00:53:21,733 --> 00:53:24,367
and tried to talk to him,
but he wouldn't answer.

1647
00:53:25,567 --> 00:53:27,433
He just lay there,
staring up at us.

1648
00:53:28,600 --> 00:53:30,167
The doctors told us
that the chemicals might

1649
00:53:30,167 --> 00:53:33,533
affect certain glands and
nerves, and if they did,

1650
00:53:33,533 --> 00:53:36,133
his features would
never be normal again.

1651
00:53:36,133 --> 00:53:37,567
- [Donelly] And you hadn't
seen him since then?

1652
00:53:37,567 --> 00:53:39,300
- No, he left the hospital
after several weeks

1653
00:53:39,300 --> 00:53:41,000
and just dropped out of sight.

1654
00:53:41,000 --> 00:53:42,833
- Did the doctors tell you
anything else about him,

1655
00:53:42,833 --> 00:53:44,567
I mean, about his mental state?

1656
00:53:44,567 --> 00:53:45,433
- Oh, yes.

1657
00:53:45,433 --> 00:53:46,900
- [Tom] He was mental.

1658
00:53:46,900 --> 00:53:47,567
- One of them did say
that Hal was pretty bitter

1659
00:53:47,567 --> 00:53:48,533
when he left.

1660
00:53:48,533 --> 00:53:49,367
- Yeah.

1661
00:53:49,367 --> 00:53:50,600
- [Crow] I wonder why.

1662
00:53:50,600 --> 00:53:51,400
- A thing like that
could very easily

1663
00:53:51,400 --> 00:53:52,400
have affected his mind.

1664
00:53:52,400 --> 00:53:54,600
And if he is the
creeper, I guess

1665
00:53:54,600 --> 00:53:55,867
that's what happened.

1666
00:53:55,867 --> 00:53:57,900
- You mean, he may have
killed Professor Cushman

1667
00:53:57,900 --> 00:54:00,333
just because he kept him
after class that day?

1668
00:54:00,333 --> 00:54:01,867
- Possibly.

1669
00:54:01,867 --> 00:54:03,100
- [Mike] I'm not telling.

1670
00:54:03,100 --> 00:54:04,100
- The mental quirk
can develop into an

1671
00:54:04,100 --> 00:54:05,567
extreme case of paranoia.

1672
00:54:05,567 --> 00:54:06,800
- [Tom] So quit looking at me.

1673
00:54:06,800 --> 00:54:08,533
- You say he was
in love with you?

1674
00:54:08,533 --> 00:54:10,300
- Well, I don't know.

1675
00:54:10,300 --> 00:54:12,200
I never took Hal very
seriously because

1676
00:54:12,200 --> 00:54:13,833
Cliff and I were in
love with each other.

1677
00:54:13,833 --> 00:54:14,700
- [Crow] It's over now.

1678
00:54:14,700 --> 00:54:16,267
- I see.

1679
00:54:16,267 --> 00:54:17,733
Well, thank you very
much for the information

1680
00:54:17,733 --> 00:54:19,100
you've given.

1681
00:54:19,100 --> 00:54:19,767
- [Mike] It was
long-winded and pointless

1682
00:54:19,767 --> 00:54:20,433
but, thank you.

1683
00:54:21,600 --> 00:54:22,867
(footsteps)

1684
00:54:22,867 --> 00:54:25,533
- [Tom] Say, do you have
a statue I could borrow?

1685
00:54:25,533 --> 00:54:26,700
(door creaking)

1686
00:54:26,700 --> 00:54:28,700
- Oh, I'm going to
have your place watched

1687
00:54:28,700 --> 00:54:30,833
day and night for
a while, Mr. Scott.

1688
00:54:30,833 --> 00:54:32,433
- You mean, he might come here?

1689
00:54:32,433 --> 00:54:34,233
- You never can tell
what a man will do

1690
00:54:34,233 --> 00:54:35,567
when his mind's affected.

1691
00:54:35,567 --> 00:54:37,700
Better to be on the safe side.

1692
00:54:37,700 --> 00:54:40,467
He might hold you responsible
for what happened.

1693
00:54:40,467 --> 00:54:42,833
After all, you gave
him the wrong answers.

1694
00:54:42,833 --> 00:54:44,433
- Yes, I did.

1695
00:54:44,433 --> 00:54:45,667
- Well, don't let it upset you.

1696
00:54:45,667 --> 00:54:47,900
We won't let anything
happen, and you will

1697
00:54:47,900 --> 00:54:49,567
explain to Mrs.
Scott about my men

1698
00:54:49,567 --> 00:54:50,800
covering the place?

1699
00:54:50,800 --> 00:54:51,800
She might think
they're prowlers.

1700
00:54:51,800 --> 00:54:52,633
- Surely.

1701
00:54:52,633 --> 00:54:53,467
- Good night.

1702
00:54:53,467 --> 00:54:55,000
- Good night.

1703
00:54:55,000 --> 00:54:56,667
- [Crow] Is there anything I
could have done differently?

1704
00:54:56,667 --> 00:54:57,633
(door closes)

1705
00:54:57,633 --> 00:54:59,100
(cars passing)

1706
00:54:59,100 --> 00:55:01,400
- [Tom] I have often walked
down this street before.

1707
00:55:01,400 --> 00:55:04,633
Feet stay beneath
the pavement before.

1708
00:55:04,633 --> 00:55:07,333
On the street where you live.

1709
00:55:07,333 --> 00:55:08,467
- [Mike] I thought
I'd look up and it'd

1710
00:55:08,467 --> 00:55:10,067
remind me of something, but no.

1711
00:55:10,933 --> 00:55:12,800
- [Crow] Yes, I'll join band.

1712
00:55:14,067 --> 00:55:17,633
(sad orchestral music)

1713
00:55:17,633 --> 00:55:18,467
- [Tom] Evening, creeper.

1714
00:55:18,467 --> 00:55:19,300
- [Crow] Hi, person.

1715
00:55:20,700 --> 00:55:24,500
(sad orchestral music)

1716
00:55:24,500 --> 00:55:25,900
River wide, oh.

1717
00:55:25,900 --> 00:55:28,967
(sad orchestral music)

1718
00:55:28,967 --> 00:55:31,133
- [Tom] Ah, the Joan
Rivers collections.

1719
00:55:31,133 --> 00:55:33,633
- [Mike] Man, how to those
things get off the ground?

1720
00:55:33,633 --> 00:55:36,200
- [Tom] That's a good question.

1721
00:55:37,367 --> 00:55:39,233
- [Crow] I'm going
to get a big facial.

1722
00:55:41,467 --> 00:55:45,633
- [Mike] How do you, what
is this, oh, it's a door.

1723
00:55:46,700 --> 00:55:47,700
- Be right with you.

1724
00:55:47,700 --> 00:55:48,800
- [Tom] I gotta lay some cable.

1725
00:55:51,333 --> 00:55:53,233
- [Mike] Have a bra
fitter on tonight?

1726
00:55:54,500 --> 00:55:58,100
(ominous orchestral music)

1727
00:55:59,800 --> 00:56:01,100
- See something
you like, mister?

1728
00:56:01,100 --> 00:56:02,867
- [Crow] Yeah, but
it not on menu.

1729
00:56:04,800 --> 00:56:06,400
- [Tom] I'm looking
for a rig to make

1730
00:56:06,400 --> 00:56:07,733
my head look dainty.

1731
00:56:08,900 --> 00:56:09,933
- That's a very fine
piece, my friend,

1732
00:56:09,933 --> 00:56:11,333
and very reasonable.

1733
00:56:12,267 --> 00:56:13,767
You can have it for--

1734
00:56:13,767 --> 00:56:15,833
- [Tom] 35 uglies,
I mean, dollars!

1735
00:56:15,833 --> 00:56:17,200
- What's the matter?

1736
00:56:17,200 --> 00:56:19,033
- Nothing, I was just
going to say that you

1737
00:56:19,033 --> 00:56:21,267
can buy it for
practically nothing.

1738
00:56:21,267 --> 00:56:22,533
- How much?

1739
00:56:22,533 --> 00:56:25,967
- To you, 12 dollars and a half.

1740
00:56:25,967 --> 00:56:27,600
- I'll take it.

1741
00:56:27,600 --> 00:56:29,000
I'll pay you for it tomorrow.

1742
00:56:29,000 --> 00:56:30,900
- Sorry, I don't do
business that way.

1743
00:56:31,767 --> 00:56:33,000
- You don't trust me?

1744
00:56:33,000 --> 00:56:34,800
- In my business,
it's too expensive.

1745
00:56:34,800 --> 00:56:35,967
- [Tom] Is he Dvorak?

1746
00:56:35,967 --> 00:56:37,633
- Put it back or
I'll call the police.

1747
00:56:39,233 --> 00:56:40,433
(grabbing)
(orchestral swell)

1748
00:56:40,433 --> 00:56:41,500
- Help! Help!
- Okay, tomorrow's

1749
00:56:41,500 --> 00:56:42,933
fine, fine, fine!

1750
00:56:45,700 --> 00:56:50,467
(car passing)
(ominous orchestral music)

1751
00:56:50,467 --> 00:56:51,300
Evening, creeper.

1752
00:56:51,300 --> 00:56:52,133
- [Tom] I, fellow.

1753
00:56:53,667 --> 00:56:56,767
- [Mike] Wow, the world
sure was dingy back then.

1754
00:56:56,767 --> 00:56:59,500
(singing)

1755
00:56:59,500 --> 00:57:03,167
(ominous orchestral music)

1756
00:57:03,167 --> 00:57:04,500
Just like Toots Thielemans.

1757
00:57:04,500 --> 00:57:05,933
- [Crow] Thank you.

1758
00:57:05,933 --> 00:57:08,267
- [Mike] Rondo Hatton
in Barefoot in the Park.

1759
00:57:09,767 --> 00:57:11,100
- [Tom] I wonder what the
odds are that I live here.

1760
00:57:11,100 --> 00:57:12,067
- [Woman] Who's there?

1761
00:57:12,067 --> 00:57:12,900
- It's me.

1762
00:57:12,900 --> 00:57:14,167
- [Crow] Mr. Creeper.

1763
00:57:14,167 --> 00:57:15,100
- [Woman] Oh, I'm glad.

1764
00:57:15,100 --> 00:57:16,600
- [Tom] No, I take that back.

1765
00:57:16,600 --> 00:57:17,433
- Come in.

1766
00:57:18,667 --> 00:57:20,233
- [Mike] Boy, what
a day I've had.

1767
00:57:20,233 --> 00:57:21,900
I gotta say, I'm
all creeped out.

1768
00:57:21,900 --> 00:57:24,233
- I was worried about you.

1769
00:57:24,233 --> 00:57:26,533
I mean, those men
who were after you.

1770
00:57:26,533 --> 00:57:28,333
- Thanks for helping me.

1771
00:57:28,333 --> 00:57:29,800
I brought you something.

1772
00:57:29,800 --> 00:57:31,300
- Oh, thank you.

1773
00:57:31,300 --> 00:57:32,133
What is it?

1774
00:57:33,367 --> 00:57:35,667
- Why don't you look at it?

1775
00:57:35,667 --> 00:57:37,700
- You see, I'm blind.

1776
00:57:37,700 --> 00:57:39,667
- [Tom] Lemon Jefferson.

1777
00:57:39,667 --> 00:57:40,500
- Blind?

1778
00:57:40,500 --> 00:57:41,967
- [Tom] Lead me on.

1779
00:57:41,967 --> 00:57:43,633
- I thought you might have
guessed it last night.

1780
00:57:44,567 --> 00:57:45,667
What did you bring me?

1781
00:57:47,400 --> 00:57:48,333
- Here.

1782
00:57:48,333 --> 00:57:49,367
- [Crow] It's a View-Master.

1783
00:57:50,567 --> 00:57:52,500
- [Tom] Did you
keep the receipt?

1784
00:57:53,533 --> 00:57:56,433
- Oh, it's beautiful,
I can tell.

1785
00:57:56,433 --> 00:57:59,167
(strained orchestral music)

1786
00:57:59,167 --> 00:58:00,733
Please, let's sit down.

1787
00:58:00,733 --> 00:58:02,800
- [Crow] Oh, does he string
krill through his mouth?

1788
00:58:02,800 --> 00:58:04,367
- [Mike] Big baleen.

1789
00:58:04,367 --> 00:58:06,767
- [Tom] So do we
make out now, or?

1790
00:58:08,200 --> 00:58:09,867
- Why were those
men chasing you?

1791
00:58:11,000 --> 00:58:12,567
- They'd thought
I'd done something,

1792
00:58:12,567 --> 00:58:14,500
but it wasn't my fault.

1793
00:58:14,500 --> 00:58:15,733
- Oh, I'm sure it wasn't.

1794
00:58:15,733 --> 00:58:18,100
- [Mike] Creeper, may
I call you "creep"?

1795
00:58:18,100 --> 00:58:20,933
- I don't know if I should
accept this from you.

1796
00:58:20,933 --> 00:58:23,000
You haven't even
told me your name.

1797
00:58:23,000 --> 00:58:24,500
- It's Hal.

1798
00:58:24,500 --> 00:58:25,667
- Mine's Helen.

1799
00:58:25,667 --> 00:58:27,000
- [Crow] We're
engaged now, right?

1800
00:58:27,000 --> 00:58:28,667
- Last night you seemed
surprised because

1801
00:58:28,667 --> 00:58:29,900
I wasn't afraid of you.

1802
00:58:31,100 --> 00:58:32,900
What's wrong, Hal?

1803
00:58:32,900 --> 00:58:33,833
Should I be afraid?

1804
00:58:33,833 --> 00:58:34,667
- [Tom] Might be a good idea.

1805
00:58:34,667 --> 00:58:36,000
- Everyone else is.

1806
00:58:36,000 --> 00:58:38,133
- Well, now I am.
- Why?

1807
00:58:38,133 --> 00:58:39,200
- I can't tell you.

1808
00:58:39,200 --> 00:58:41,033
- [Crow] It's a surprise.

1809
00:58:41,033 --> 00:58:42,533
- Do you wanna be
friends with me?

1810
00:58:42,533 --> 00:58:45,067
- [Tom] Gee, I don't know,
I have so many, well, okay.

1811
00:58:45,067 --> 00:58:46,367
- Sure, you're nice to me.

1812
00:58:47,933 --> 00:58:50,900
- And no one else
is, is that it?

1813
00:58:50,900 --> 00:58:52,367
- [Tom] The coy Rondo Hatton.

1814
00:58:54,200 --> 00:58:55,467
- I think I understand.

1815
00:58:55,467 --> 00:58:56,900
- [Crow] You're a
hideous murderer.

1816
00:58:58,067 --> 00:59:00,567
- When you've been
blind as long as I have

1817
00:59:00,567 --> 00:59:02,333
you learn to see
through your senses.

1818
00:59:02,333 --> 00:59:04,067
- [Mike] I sense
through my sweat glands.

1819
00:59:04,067 --> 00:59:07,100
- I can't explain it exactly,
but you get a feeling

1820
00:59:07,100 --> 00:59:08,700
about people when you meet them.

1821
00:59:09,933 --> 00:59:12,400
You see a picture of
them in your mind.

1822
00:59:12,400 --> 00:59:13,833
- [Tom] Picturing a grouper.

1823
00:59:13,833 --> 00:59:15,700
- Not just what they look
like, but what they really are.

1824
00:59:15,700 --> 00:59:17,700
- [Crow] And what
size hat they wear.

1825
00:59:17,700 --> 00:59:18,933
- You see, them
much more clearly

1826
00:59:18,933 --> 00:59:20,500
then you do with your eyes.

1827
00:59:20,500 --> 00:59:22,000
- [Tom] No, you don't.

1828
00:59:22,000 --> 00:59:24,133
- Maybe that's why they
say "looks are deceptive".

1829
00:59:24,133 --> 00:59:25,600
- [Tom] No, they're not.

1830
00:59:25,600 --> 00:59:26,533
- Do you know what I mean?

1831
00:59:26,533 --> 00:59:28,133
- [Crow] You're smothering me.

1832
00:59:28,133 --> 00:59:30,500
- Yes, I know.

1833
00:59:30,500 --> 00:59:32,700
- I'd like to help
you, if you'd let me.

1834
00:59:32,700 --> 00:59:34,000
- You can.

1835
00:59:34,000 --> 00:59:34,933
- How?

1836
00:59:34,933 --> 00:59:36,867
- Just let me come to see you.

1837
00:59:36,867 --> 00:59:39,033
- Of course, I want you to.

1838
00:59:39,033 --> 00:59:40,600
I'm alone most of
the time, except

1839
00:59:40,600 --> 00:59:41,833
when I give piano lessons.

1840
00:59:41,833 --> 00:59:43,700
- [Tom] Or when I'm
at umpire school.

1841
00:59:43,700 --> 00:59:46,600
- I have such a good
idea of you now,

1842
00:59:46,600 --> 00:59:48,233
but if I could touch
your face, then--

1843
00:59:48,233 --> 00:59:50,600
- No, don't do that.

1844
00:59:50,600 --> 00:59:51,900
- Why not?

1845
00:59:51,900 --> 00:59:52,967
- I don't want you to.

1846
00:59:52,967 --> 00:59:54,867
- [Crow] I'm breaking out, okay?

1847
00:59:54,867 --> 00:59:56,367
- Hal.

1848
00:59:56,367 --> 00:59:57,733
- [Mike] You're wearing
Drakkar Noir, aren't you?

1849
00:59:58,567 --> 00:59:59,800
(door shuts)
- Hal.

1850
00:59:59,800 --> 01:00:01,500
- [Tom] Open the
pod bay door, Hal.

1851
01:00:02,600 --> 01:00:04,900
Hmm, Miss Helen Creeper.

1852
01:00:06,033 --> 01:00:08,733
(strained orchestra music)

1853
01:00:08,733 --> 01:00:09,833
(moves to ominous
orchestral music)

1854
01:00:09,833 --> 01:00:11,500
- [Crow] Refused
to use articles.

1855
01:00:13,033 --> 01:00:14,167
- [Tom] A police wrong.

1856
01:00:16,000 --> 01:00:19,433
(scary orchestra music)

1857
01:00:19,433 --> 01:00:20,933
- [Crow] Or your money back!

1858
01:00:21,867 --> 01:00:23,900
(ominous orchestral music)

1859
01:00:23,900 --> 01:00:25,600
- [Mike] The haunting
creeper theme.

1860
01:00:27,300 --> 01:00:28,867
- [Crow] Come on, I
didn't mean to break it.

1861
01:00:28,867 --> 01:00:30,100
I thought it was in park.

1862
01:00:34,667 --> 01:00:35,600
- Well, good morning,
Commissioner.

1863
01:00:35,600 --> 01:00:37,933
Good morning, Mr. Parkington.

1864
01:00:37,933 --> 01:00:40,200
I thought you'd be dropping
by about this time.

1865
01:00:40,200 --> 01:00:41,867
- So, you're sitting
here playing Gin Rummy

1866
01:00:41,867 --> 01:00:43,433
with another creeper
murder on the front page

1867
01:00:43,433 --> 01:00:44,700
of the newspapers.

1868
01:00:44,700 --> 01:00:47,100
- I think better when
I'm enjoy my hobby.

1869
01:00:47,100 --> 01:00:48,233
I'm a pigeon fancier.

1870
01:00:49,700 --> 01:00:50,967
- [Tom] What is this scene for?

1871
01:00:50,967 --> 01:00:52,233
- Now, listen to me,
Donelly, we told you--

1872
01:00:52,233 --> 01:00:53,767
- I know, I know,
you told me already

1873
01:00:53,767 --> 01:00:55,900
that you wanted the creeper
arrested within 24 hours.

1874
01:00:55,900 --> 01:00:58,800
Well, gentlemen,
he's still at large.

1875
01:00:58,800 --> 01:01:00,967
- Now, I suppose the bear
and yourself have decided

1876
01:01:00,967 --> 01:01:02,667
to take over, personally.

1877
01:01:02,667 --> 01:01:04,533
No doubt, you'll have
the killer in custody in

1878
01:01:04,533 --> 01:01:06,200
10 minutes flat.

1879
01:01:06,200 --> 01:01:08,233
I'll just let the newspapers
know that it's in your lap.

1880
01:01:08,233 --> 01:01:09,633
- Now, just a minute,
Captain, let's not

1881
01:01:09,633 --> 01:01:10,900
be hasty about this.

1882
01:01:10,900 --> 01:01:12,400
- You don't mean, that
his honor, the mayor,

1883
01:01:12,400 --> 01:01:14,300
wouldn't relish the job,
that you wouldn't like

1884
01:01:14,300 --> 01:01:15,533
to take over?

1885
01:01:15,533 --> 01:01:16,967
- That's beside the
point, the entire city

1886
01:01:16,967 --> 01:01:19,100
is up in arms, and the creeper
goes on killing people.

1887
01:01:19,100 --> 01:01:21,267
Now, I'm a reasonable
man, Donelly.

1888
01:01:21,267 --> 01:01:22,667
- I'm glad to hear
it, Commissioner.

1889
01:01:22,667 --> 01:01:24,167
- [Mike] It's uncle Charlie.

1890
01:01:24,167 --> 01:01:25,367
- Now that the pressure's
off, I can let you know

1891
01:01:25,367 --> 01:01:27,100
that we have the
first definite clues

1892
01:01:27,100 --> 01:01:28,500
to the identity of the creeper.

1893
01:01:28,500 --> 01:01:29,333
- Who is it?

1894
01:01:29,333 --> 01:01:30,267
- Yes, who is it?

1895
01:01:30,267 --> 01:01:31,700
- I said "clue", Gentlemen.

1896
01:01:31,700 --> 01:01:33,600
When I'm certain, I'll
give you the lowdown,

1897
01:01:33,600 --> 01:01:35,600
and I believe it'll be soon.

1898
01:01:35,600 --> 01:01:37,667
That's all I can tell
you at the moment.

1899
01:01:37,667 --> 01:01:40,433
Now, if you'll excuse
me, I have Gates on the

1900
01:01:40,433 --> 01:01:41,600
triple blitz.

1901
01:01:41,600 --> 01:01:42,533
- [Crow] I'm beginning
to lose my trust

1902
01:01:42,533 --> 01:01:43,867
in government.

1903
01:01:43,867 --> 01:01:45,067
- [Tom] Nice to know
that Noel Coward's

1904
01:01:45,067 --> 01:01:47,300
in charge of the police force.

1905
01:01:47,300 --> 01:01:48,267
(door closes)

1906
01:01:48,267 --> 01:01:49,233
- Why didn't you let them know

1907
01:01:49,233 --> 01:01:50,433
about the newspaper clippings?

1908
01:01:50,433 --> 01:01:52,000
- What, and have
those publicity hounds

1909
01:01:52,000 --> 01:01:53,633
filling the front pages
with a lot of stuff

1910
01:01:53,633 --> 01:01:55,900
that would scare the
creeper right out of town?

1911
01:01:57,667 --> 01:02:00,500
(synth pop music)

1912
01:02:03,533 --> 01:02:05,933
(moves to ominous
orchestral music)

1913
01:02:05,933 --> 01:02:08,100
- [Mike] Well, that sex
was very serviceable

1914
01:02:08,100 --> 01:02:09,833
and reliable, thank you.

1915
01:02:09,833 --> 01:02:11,400
- [Tom] Why, hello, creeper.

1916
01:02:11,400 --> 01:02:12,233
- [Crow] Hi, creeper.

1917
01:02:12,233 --> 01:02:13,067
- [Tom] Hi.

1918
01:02:14,200 --> 01:02:15,933
(ominous orchestral music)

1919
01:02:15,933 --> 01:02:17,033
What, did this take
place in Alaska?

1920
01:02:17,033 --> 01:02:18,300
There's no daylight.

1921
01:02:18,300 --> 01:02:19,967
- [Crow] I can't believe
she makes me go outside

1922
01:02:19,967 --> 01:02:21,133
to smoke a cigarette.

1923
01:02:23,300 --> 01:02:25,300
- [Mike] I'd better go
drop off the instructions

1924
01:02:25,300 --> 01:02:26,967
for the brooch.

1925
01:02:26,967 --> 01:02:29,367
- [Tom] Welcome to the
creeper arms apartments.

1926
01:02:30,400 --> 01:02:33,733
(ominous orchestral music)

1927
01:02:33,733 --> 01:02:37,300
- One and two and
three and four and--

1928
01:02:37,300 --> 01:02:39,600
- [Crow] She's not a
very good singer, is she?

1929
01:02:39,600 --> 01:02:41,133
- [Mike] The song's
got something to do

1930
01:02:41,133 --> 01:02:42,400
with it, too.

1931
01:02:42,400 --> 01:02:45,233
- One and two and
three and four and

1932
01:02:45,233 --> 01:02:48,667
one and two and
three and four and,

1933
01:02:48,667 --> 01:02:52,100
that's find, Dorothy, just fine.

1934
01:02:52,100 --> 01:02:53,600
- [Mike] That'll be 600 dollars.

1935
01:02:53,600 --> 01:02:55,367
- She's coming along very
nicely, Mrs. Obringer.

1936
01:02:55,367 --> 01:02:56,867
- [Tom] She is?

1937
01:02:56,867 --> 01:02:57,833
- You'll be playing the
classics before you know it.

1938
01:02:57,833 --> 01:02:59,067
- I don't wanna play that stuff,

1939
01:02:59,067 --> 01:03:00,400
I wanna play boogie-woogie.

1940
01:03:00,400 --> 01:03:03,033
- Well, you can play
that, too, as a hobby,

1941
01:03:03,033 --> 01:03:04,633
but first, you must
master the things

1942
01:03:04,633 --> 01:03:05,900
that are solid.

1943
01:03:05,900 --> 01:03:08,833
- But boogie-woogie is
solid, really sensual.

1944
01:03:08,833 --> 01:03:10,900
- Hush, dear.

1945
01:03:10,900 --> 01:03:13,467
Put your music away,
we're going home now.

1946
01:03:13,467 --> 01:03:15,233
She get's that from
her father, he plays

1947
01:03:15,233 --> 01:03:17,633
hot trumpet in the
fireman's band.

1948
01:03:17,633 --> 01:03:19,233
Come along, dear.

1949
01:03:20,400 --> 01:03:21,900
- [Tom] Why do you
not open, door?

1950
01:03:21,900 --> 01:03:23,133
(door opens)

1951
01:03:23,133 --> 01:03:24,767
- We'll see you next
week at the same time,

1952
01:03:24,767 --> 01:03:26,367
I'll see that she
practices like you

1953
01:03:26,367 --> 01:03:27,867
told her to, Miss Helen.

1954
01:03:27,867 --> 01:03:29,167
- I'll be right in the groove.

1955
01:03:29,167 --> 01:03:30,100
- Oh, Dorothy.

1956
01:03:31,033 --> 01:03:31,867
Good night.

1957
01:03:31,867 --> 01:03:32,967
- Good night.

1958
01:03:34,100 --> 01:03:35,667
- [Crow] Hey, fellow,
why the long face?

1959
01:03:35,667 --> 01:03:36,600
- [Mike] Oh, now, come on.

1960
01:03:36,600 --> 01:03:37,833
- [Crow] (laughing) I'm sorry.

1961
01:03:37,833 --> 01:03:38,767
- [Mike] I begged
you not to do that.

1962
01:03:38,767 --> 01:03:39,600
- [Crow] No.

1963
01:03:39,600 --> 01:03:40,533
- [Tom] That hurts.

1964
01:03:41,633 --> 01:03:43,700
If the hands of time.

1965
01:03:43,700 --> 01:03:45,100
(sad piano music)

1966
01:03:45,100 --> 01:03:46,367
- [Mike] You have a
real problem with that.

1967
01:03:48,733 --> 01:03:49,800
- [Crow] Hi, Amaire.

1968
01:03:51,800 --> 01:03:53,433
- [Tom] Uh, you want to
use a different fingering

1969
01:03:53,433 --> 01:03:55,633
not to break up the phrasing.

1970
01:03:55,633 --> 01:03:58,467
Uh, look, Helen, other
girls in the sorority

1971
01:03:58,467 --> 01:04:00,333
asked me to talk to you
about your incessant

1972
01:04:00,333 --> 01:04:01,567
piano playing.

1973
01:04:01,567 --> 01:04:02,667
- [Crow] She can't hear him?

1974
01:04:02,667 --> 01:04:04,500
She's the most
inefficient blind person

